QB.add(34,[{"id":34001,"t":"Infection and Human Disease","s":"A global-health researcher compares the WHO top-10 causes of death in 2019 worldwide with those in low-income countries. Which finding best summarises the difference?","o":["Infection tops the list only in low-income countries","Ischaemic heart disease is absent from the low-income top ten","Tuberculosis is the leading cause of death in both settings","Diarrhoeal disease outranks stroke in both settings","Infections no longer appear in the global top ten at all"],"e":"Globally the top three causes are ischaemic heart disease, stroke and COPD, all non-communicable. In low-income countries neonatal conditions and lower respiratory infections (communicable) are first and second, ahead of ischaemic heart disease. Lower respiratory infections and diarrhoeal disease still appear in the global list, so infection has not vanished; the gap reflects inequality in healthcare.","w":["Ischaemic heart disease is third in the low-income list.","Tuberculosis is eighth in low-income countries and absent from the global top ten.","Globally stroke is second and diarrhoeal disease eighth.","Lower respiratory infections are fourth globally and diarrhoeal disease eighth."],"k":"Infectious causes dominate the top of the low-income mortality list, not the global list.","rn":[["","What are the top 3 causes of death in low-income countries?","What are the top 3 causes of death in low-income countries?","Neonatal conditions; Lower respiratory infections; Ischaemic heart disease"],["","Which infections rank high in low income countries?","Which infections rank high in low income countries?","Lower respiratory infections; Diarrhoeal diseases; Malaria"]]},{"id":34002,"t":"Infection and Human Disease","s":"Which pair of historical pandemics is correctly matched with its causative organism and estimated mortality?","o":["Black death: <i>Yersinia pestis</i>, ~30% of Europeans; White death: <i>M. tuberculosis</i>, up to 25% of the UK","Black death: <i>M. tuberculosis</i>, ~30% of Europeans; White death: <i>Y. pestis</i>, up to 25% of the UK","Black death: <i>Y. pestis</i>, up to 25% of the UK; White death: <i>M. tuberculosis</i>, ~30% of Europeans","Black death: <i>Bacillus anthracis</i>, ~30% of Europeans; White death: <i>M. leprae</i>, up to 25% of the UK","Black death: <i>Y. pestis</i>, ~10% of Europeans; White death: <i>M. bovis</i>, up to 50% of the UK"],"e":"Plague (the Black death, <i>Yersinia pestis</i>) killed about 30% of the European population in the 16th–17th centuries. Tuberculosis (the White death/plague, <i>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</i>) killed up to 25% of the UK population in the 18th–19th centuries. Survivors of plague who carried protective gene variants passed them on, an example of infection shaping human evolution.","w":["The organisms are swapped.","The percentages are swapped between the two diseases.","<i>B. anthracis</i> causes anthrax and <i>M. leprae</i> leprosy, not these pandemics.","The figures and the mycobacterium are wrong."],"k":"Plague = Y. pestis (~30% Europe); TB = M. tuberculosis (up to 25% UK).","rn":[["","What causes plague (\"Black death\")?","What causes plague (\"Black death\")?","Yersinia pestis"],["","How much of the UK did tuberculosis (\"White death\") kill?","How much of the UK did tuberculosis (\"White death\") kill?","Up to 25%; 18th-19th centuries"]]},{"id":34003,"t":"Infection and Human Disease","s":"A 68-year-old develops diarrhoea on day 6 of a hospital admission for hip replacement. Infection acquired in this way is best described as:","a":["nosocomial","nosocomial infection","hospital acquired","hospital-acquired","hospital acquired infection","hai"],"e":"Infections acquired in hospital are nosocomial. Examples include <i>S. aureus</i> (including MRSA), <i>Enterococcus</i> (including VRE), <i>Clostridium difficile</i>, nosocomial pneumonia, wound infections and implant/device-related infections. Surgery creates a wound, an entry route for microorganisms, which is why peri-operative antibiotics were transformative.","k":"Hospital-acquired infection = nosocomial.","rn":[["","What is Nosocomial infection?","What is Nosocomial infection?","Infection acquired in hospital"]]},{"id":34004,"t":"Infection and Human Disease","s":"Why did the introduction of antibiotics transform the outcome of surgery, and why is that gain now under threat?","o":["Wounds let microbes in; resistance now erodes that protection","Antibiotics sterilise theatres; resistance has made operating theatres unsafe to sterilise","Antibiotics replace the immune system after surgery; patients now lack immune cells","Surgical wounds only become infected by viruses; new viruses now evade antivirals","Antibiotics prevent anaesthetic toxicity; resistance now makes anaesthesia more dangerous"],"e":"However successful an operation, the wound is an entry route for organisms such as <i>S. aureus</i>, so infection used to be common and often fatal. Antibiotics given before and after surgery largely removed this. Some of these organisms (MRSA, VRE) are now resistant, new antibiotics are hard to develop and development has plateaued, while microbes adapt faster than new drugs appear.","w":["Antibiotics treat infection in the patient; they do not sterilise rooms.","Antibiotics support, not replace, host defence.","The listed nosocomial organisms are mainly bacteria.","Antibiotics do not act on anaesthetic toxicity."],"k":"Peri-operative antibiotics prevent wound infection; resistance threatens this.","rn":[["","Before antibiotics, why was surgery risky?","Before antibiotics, why was surgery risky?","Wound let infection in; Death even if surgery succeeded"]]},{"id":34005,"t":"Infection and Human Disease","s":"Which statement about the human microbiome is correct?","o":["All microbial genes on and in the body: ~200× the human genome's genes","It is the total number of bacterial cells in the gut, roughly equal to the number of human cells","It includes only bacteria living in the large intestine, with about 20 times more genes than human cells","It is the set of pathogenic organisms carried by an individual at any time","It is the human genes that encode receptors for microbial molecules"],"e":"The microbiome is the genetic material of all the microbes (bacteria, fungi, protozoa and viruses) that live on and inside the body. The genes in one person's microbiome outnumber those of the human genome about 200-fold. Most of these microbes are protective, competing with harmful organisms for nutrients and space.","w":["It is defined by genes, not cell number, and includes all body sites.","It includes fungi, protozoa and viruses at all sites, and the factor is 200.","Most microbiome organisms are not pathogens.","It is microbial, not human, genetic material."],"k":"Microbiome = all microbial genes on/in us; ~200× human gene count.","rn":[["","Microbiome genes vs human genome genes?","Microbiome genes vs human genome genes?","Microbiome has 200 times more"]]},{"id":34006,"t":"Infection and Human Disease","s":"A patient on prolonged broad-spectrum antibiotics develops a new inflammatory condition linked in research to disturbance of the gut microbiota. Which group of conditions has been associated with an altered microbiome?","o":["Obesity, IBD, liver disease and diabetes mellitus","Haemophilia, sickle cell disease and cystic fibrosis","Fractures, sprains and tendon rupture","Cataract, glaucoma and myopia","Down syndrome, Turner syndrome and Klinefelter syndrome"],"e":"Altered microbiota has been linked to obesity, inflammatory bowel disease (IBD), liver disease (cirrhosis, ALD, NAFLD), emerging infectious disease, diabetes mellitus, atherosclerosis and metabolic syndrome, largely metabolic disorders. Normal flora protect us by competing with harmful microorganisms for nutrients and environment, so disturbing them can tip the balance.","w":["These are inherited single-gene disorders.","These are mechanical injuries.","These eye conditions are not on the microbiome list.","These are chromosomal disorders."],"k":"Microbiome disturbance links to metabolic disease, IBD, liver disease.","rn":[["","Altered microbiome: 3 metabolic diseases?","Altered microbiome: 3 metabolic diseases?","Obesity; Diabetes mellitus; Metabolic syndrome"],["","Altered microbiome: 4 other diseases?","Altered microbiome: 4 other diseases?","IBD; Liver disease; Atherosclerosis; Emerging infections"],["","Give 3 diseases from a disturbed microbiome","Give 3 diseases from a disturbed microbiome","Metabolic syndrome; Obesity; Liver disease"]]},{"id":34007,"t":"Infection and Human Disease","s":"Why can the normal resident flora reduce the risk of infection by pathogens?","o":["They compete with pathogens for nutrients and niches","They secrete antibodies that neutralise incoming pathogens","They form a continuous peptidoglycan layer over the epithelium","They prevent the host making any inflammatory response","They convert pathogens into commensals by gene transfer"],"e":"Most microbes living on us are beneficial: they occupy the space and use the food that harmful microorganisms would otherwise exploit, acting as “invisible soldiers”. They can become harmful in some situations, but in general this competition protects the host. Prokaryotes also protect skin and epithelial tissue from invasion and help digestion.","w":["Antibodies are made by the host's B cells, not by bacteria.","Peptidoglycan forms each bacterium's own wall, not a host covering.","Normal flora do not switch off host inflammation.","Gene transfer does not turn pathogens into commensals as a protective mechanism."],"k":"Normal flora protect by competition for nutrients and space."},{"id":34008,"t":"Koch's Postulates","s":"Researchers isolate a new bacterium from every patient with an unusual pneumonia and grow it in pure culture. Which further step is required to satisfy Koch's postulates?","o":["Reproduce the disease in a healthy host, then re-isolate it","Show the organism is Gram negative and carries lipopolysaccharide","Show patients produce antibodies to the organism","Show the organism is resistant to the antibiotics used","Show that the organism is absent from the hospital environment"],"e":"Koch's postulates: (1) a specific microorganism is always associated with the disease; (2) it can be isolated and grown in pure culture; (3) the cultured microbe causes disease when transferred to a healthy animal (today, patients replace animals in reasoning); (4) the same organism can be re-isolated from the newly infected host. All four must be met.","w":["Gram reaction does not establish causation.","An antibody response is not one of Koch's four criteria.","Resistance is irrelevant to causation.","Environmental absence is not one of the postulates."],"k":"Koch: associated → pure culture → reproduce disease → re-isolate.","rn":[["","Koch's postulates 3 and 4?","Koch's postulates 3 and 4?","Causes disease in healthy animal; Re-isolated from new host"]]},{"id":34009,"t":"Infection and Human Disease","s":"Which statement violates one of Koch's postulates as originally stated?","o":["The organism is found in only some patients with the disease","The organism grows alone on a laboratory culture plate","Inoculating the cultured organism makes a healthy animal ill","The organism is recovered from the experimentally infected animal","The organism causes the same disease in the new host as in the original"],"e":"The first postulate requires that a specific microorganism is always associated with the given disease, so finding it in only some patients breaks the criterion. The other options describe pure culture (postulate 2), reproduction of disease in a healthy host (3) and re-isolation (4), which are all requirements.","w":["Pure culture is postulate 2.","This is postulate 3.","This is postulate 4.","Causing the same disease is part of postulate 3."],"k":"Postulate 1: always associated with the disease.","rn":[["","What is infection?","What is infection?","Invasion of host tissues; By foreign organisms; Maybe disease, maybe not"]]},{"id":34010,"t":"Koch's Postulates","s":"Robert Koch was awarded the Nobel Prize (1905) for proving which organism was the cause of tuberculosis? Give the full genus and species.","a":["mycobacterium tuberculosis","m tuberculosis","m. tuberculosis"],"e":"Koch proved <i>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</i> causes tuberculosis and also linked <i>Bacillus anthracis</i> to anthrax. In 1876 he established that microbes can cause disease, replacing theories such as spontaneous generation, divine punishment and “miasma” (bad air).","k":"Koch: M. tuberculosis → TB; B. anthracis → anthrax.","rn":[["","Koch proved which 2 organisms cause which diseases?","Koch proved which 2 organisms cause which diseases?","Mycobacterium tuberculosis: tuberculosis; Bacillus anthracis: anthrax"]]},{"id":34011,"t":"Koch's Postulates","s":"Which experiment provided the strongest evidence against spontaneous generation?","o":["Boiled broth stayed sterile when air was filtered","Microscopes revealed living forms too small to see unaided","Grapes fermented into wine when left in sealed containers","Animals injected with blood from sick animals became ill","Milk curdled faster at warm than at cold temperatures"],"e":"Spontaneous generation held that life forms from non-living matter. Spallanzani found boiling sterilised broth and microbes reappeared only on exposure to air; Pasteur then exposed boiled broths to air through a filter that stopped all particles reaching the medium, and no growth occurred. Van Leeuwenhoek showed microscopic life existed but did not link it to these processes.","w":["Van Leeuwenhoek's observations showed microbes exist but did not disprove spontaneous generation.","Fermentation alone does not address where the organisms came from.","This relates to transmission of disease, not the origin of microbes.","Temperature effects on curdling do not test spontaneous generation."],"k":"Filtered boiled broth stays sterile → no spontaneous generation.","rn":[["","What did Pasteur do with boiled broths?","What did Pasteur do with boiled broths?","Exposed to air via filter; Filter stopped particles"]]},{"id":34012,"t":"Types of Microorganisms","s":"Which classification of microorganisms is correct?","o":["Archaea are prokaryotes; protists are eukaryotes","Fungi are prokaryotes; bacteria are eukaryotes","Archaea and fungi are both eukaryotes","Protists are prokaryotes like bacteria","Viruses are prokaryotes without ribosomes"],"e":"The main groups are viruses; prokaryotes (bacteria and archaea); and eukaryotes (fungi and protists), with helminths as larger eukaryotic parasites. Clinically we are mostly interested in bacteria among prokaryotes and fungi and protists among eukaryotes.","w":["Fungi are eukaryotes and bacteria prokaryotes.","Archaea are prokaryotes.","Protists are eukaryotes.","Viruses are a separate group, not prokaryotes."],"k":"Prokaryotes = bacteria, archaea; eukaryotes = fungi, protists.","rn":[["","Which prokaryotes and eukaryotes matter most in infectious disease?","Which prokaryotes and eukaryotes matter most in infectious disease?","Prokaryotes: bacteria; Eukaryotes: fungi and protists"],["","What are the 3 main types of microorganism?","What are the 3 main types of microorganism?","Viruses; Prokaryotes; Eukaryotes"]]},{"id":34013,"t":"Types of Microorganisms","s":"A drug selectively damages a structure present in human cells but absent from bacteria. Which structure could it be?","o":["Mitochondria","Ribosomes","Plasma membrane","Cytoplasm","DNA"],"e":"Prokaryotes are simple unicellular organisms lacking a defined nucleus, mitochondria or other membrane-bound organelles; their DNA lies in the nucleoid. They do have ribosomes (70S), a plasma membrane, cytoplasm and DNA. Such differences between prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells are what make selective antimicrobial targets possible.","w":["Bacteria have ribosomes (70S).","All cells have a plasma membrane.","All cells have cytoplasm.","Bacteria have DNA, in a nucleoid."],"k":"Prokaryotes lack mitochondria and membrane-bound organelles.","rn":[["","What are prokaryotes?","What are prokaryotes?","Simple unicellular organisms; No nucleus, mitochondria; No membrane-bound organelles"]]},{"id":34014,"t":"Types of Microorganisms","s":"In a bacterium, the region of cytoplasm that contains the chromosomal DNA, not enclosed by a membrane, is called the:","a":["nucleoid","nucleoid region"],"e":"Unlike eukaryotes, bacterial DNA is not enclosed by a nuclear membrane; it sits in the nucleoid. Because of this, replication, transcription and translation happen next to each other, and DNA can be exchanged more readily between the chromosome and mobile elements such as plasmids and transposons.","k":"Bacterial DNA lies in the nucleoid.","rn":[["","Where is prokaryotic DNA found?","Where is prokaryotic DNA found?","Nucleoid"]]},{"id":34015,"t":"Types of Microorganisms","s":"Prokaryotes have been found in hot springs at 122 °C, in ice at −22 °C and 10 km deep in the ocean. Which other statement about prokaryotes is correct?","o":["Most are highly beneficial, e.g. aiding digestion and protecting epithelia","Most species are pathogenic to humans","They reproduce slowly compared with human cells, so they rarely outgrow the host","They all require oxygen to survive","They cannot survive outside a host, so they are never found in extreme environments"],"e":"Prokaryotes are everywhere and have rapid reproduction rates. Most are highly beneficial: they protect skin and epithelial tissue from invasion, help food digestion and are used in biotechnology. Only some are pathogenic, and many are anaerobes.","w":["Only some are pathogenic.","Prokaryotes have rapid reproduction rates.","Obligate anaerobes are poisoned by oxygen.","They live in extreme free-living environments such as hot springs and ice."],"k":"Prokaryotes: ubiquitous, fast-reproducing, mostly beneficial.","rn":[["","What are the 3 ways most prokaryotes are beneficial?","What are the 3 ways most prokaryotes are beneficial?","Protect skin/epithelium from invasion; Help food digestion; Biotechnology industry"]]},{"id":34016,"t":"Types of Microorganisms","s":"A sputum sample grows a multicellular fungus that reproduces by spores. Which organism fits best?","o":["<i>Aspergillus fumigatus</i>","<i>Candida albicans</i>","<i>Giardia lamblia</i>","<i>Plasmodium falciparum</i>","<i>Entamoeba histolytica</i>"],"e":"<i>Aspergillus</i> species (e.g. <i>fumigatus</i>) are moulds: multicellular and reproducing by spores; it is known as the common mould. <i>Candida</i> species (e.g. <i>albicans</i>) are yeasts: unicellular and reproducing by budding. The other options are protozoa.","w":["<i>Candida</i> is a unicellular yeast that buds.","<i>Giardia</i> is a protozoan.","<i>Plasmodium</i> is a protozoan malaria parasite.","<i>Entamoeba</i> is a protozoan causing amoebic dysentery."],"k":"Aspergillus = multicellular mould, spores; Candida = yeast, budding.","rn":[["","Candida vs Aspergillus","Candida vs Aspergillus","Candida: yeast, unicellular, budding; Aspergillus: mould, multicellular, spores"],["","Candida vs Aspergillus: yeast or mould?","Candida vs Aspergillus: yeast or mould?","Candida: yeast; Aspergillus: mould"]]},{"id":34017,"t":"Types of Microorganisms","s":"<i>Candida albicans</i> differs from <i>Aspergillus fumigatus</i> because it:","o":["is unicellular and reproduces by budding","is multicellular and reproduces by spores","is a prokaryote lacking a nucleus","is a protozoan spread by mosquitoes","has a peptidoglycan cell wall"],"e":"<i>Candida</i> is a yeast: unicellular, reproducing by budding; some species can be pathogenic. <i>Aspergillus</i> is a mould: multicellular, reproducing by spores. Both are eukaryotic fungi; peptidoglycan is a bacterial feature.","w":["That describes <i>Aspergillus</i>.","Fungi are eukaryotes.","That describes <i>Plasmodium</i>.","Peptidoglycan is found in bacteria."],"k":"Yeast vs mould: budding/unicellular vs spores/multicellular.","rn":[["","Candida vs Aspergillus","Candida vs Aspergillus","Candida: yeast, unicellular, budding; Aspergillus: mould, multicellular, spores"]]},{"id":34018,"t":"Types of Microorganisms","s":"A traveller returns from sub-Saharan Africa with cyclical fevers. A single-celled eukaryotic parasite transmitted by a mosquito vector is suspected. Name the organism (genus and species).","a":["plasmodium falciparum","p falciparum","p. falciparum","plasmodium"],"e":"<i>Plasmodium falciparum</i>, the malaria parasite, is a protozoan (single-celled eukaryotic parasite) that is vector-borne, using the mosquito as its vector. Protozoa cause major tropical and zoonotic diseases; <i>Giardia lamblia</i> spreads between humans and animals and <i>Entamoeba histolytica</i> causes amoebic dysentery.","k":"Malaria = P. falciparum, mosquito vector.","rn":[["","How is Plasmodium falciparum spread?","How is Plasmodium falciparum spread?","Vector borne (mosquito)"],["","What does Plasmodium falciparum use to spread?","What does Plasmodium falciparum use to spread?","Mosquito vector"],["","Name 3 protozoa.","Name 3 protozoa.","Giardia lamblia; Plasmodium falciparum (malaria); Entamoeba histolytica (amoebic dysentery)"]]},{"id":34019,"t":"Types of Microorganisms","s":"A patient has bloody diarrhoea after drinking contaminated water abroad, and the cause is a protozoan named on the slide for causing amoebic dysentery. Which organism is it?","o":["<i>Entamoeba histolytica</i>","<i>Giardia lamblia</i>","<i>Plasmodium falciparum</i>","<i>Loa loa</i>","<i>Taenia saginata</i>"],"e":"<i>Entamoeba histolytica</i> is the protozoan cause of amoebic dysentery. <i>Giardia lamblia</i> is another protozoan, highlighted for spreading between humans and animals (zoonotic). <i>Loa loa</i> and <i>Taenia saginata</i> are helminths, not protozoa.","w":["<i>Giardia</i> is a protozoan, but the amoebic dysentery organism is <i>Entamoeba</i>.","<i>Plasmodium</i> causes malaria.","<i>Loa loa</i> is the African eyeworm, a helminth.","<i>T. saginata</i> is the beef tapeworm."],"k":"Amoebic dysentery = Entamoeba histolytica.","rn":[["","Entamoeba histolytica causes what?","Entamoeba histolytica causes what?","Amoebic dysentery"],["","Name 3 protozoa.","Name 3 protozoa.","Giardia lamblia; Plasmodium falciparum (malaria); Entamoeba histolytica (amoebic dysentery)"]]},{"id":34020,"t":"Types of Microorganisms","s":"A disease that is spread between animals and humans is described as:","o":["zoonotic","nosocomial","commensal","endogenous","opportunistic"],"e":"Diseases spread between animals and humans are zoonotic; many protozoal and helminth diseases are tropical and zoonotic (e.g. <i>Giardia</i>, <i>Loa loa</i>, <i>Taenia saginata</i>). A vector, such as a mosquito for <i>Plasmodium</i> or flies for <i>Loa loa</i>, is an organism that carries the parasite to humans.","w":["Nosocomial means hospital-acquired.","Commensal describes harmless resident organisms.","Endogenous means arising from within.","Opportunistic describes organisms exploiting weakened hosts."],"k":"Zoonotic = animal-to-human spread.","rn":[["","What is a zoonotic disease?","What is a zoonotic disease?","Spread between humans and animals"],["","How are Giardia spread?","How are Giardia spread?","Between humans and animals (zoonotic)"]]},{"id":34021,"t":"Types of Microorganisms","s":"A patient from Central Africa notices a worm moving across the conjunctiva. The parasite is a fly-borne helminth. Which organism is this?","o":["<i>Loa loa</i>","<i>Taenia saginata</i>","<i>Giardia lamblia</i>","<i>Entamoeba histolytica</i>","<i>Aspergillus fumigatus</i>"],"e":"<i>Loa loa</i>, the African eyeworm, is a helminth (eukaryotic parasite) with flies as its vector. Helminths cause major tropical and zoonotic diseases. <i>Taenia saginata</i>, the beef tapeworm, is large enough to see and lives in the gut.","w":["<i>T. saginata</i> is the beef tapeworm living in the gut.","<i>Giardia</i> is a protozoan.","<i>Entamoeba</i> is a protozoan causing dysentery.","<i>Aspergillus</i> is a mould."],"k":"African eyeworm = Loa loa (fly vector).","rn":[["","Name 2 helminths.","Name 2 helminths.","Loa loa (African eyeworm); Taenia saginata (beef tapeworm)"],["","Loa loa is also called what?","Loa loa is also called what?","African eyeworm"],["","What is the vector of Loa loa?","What is the vector of Loa loa?","Flies"]]},{"id":34022,"t":"Types of Microorganisms","s":"On a logarithmic size scale, which is the best estimate of a typical bacterium and of the structure it most resembles in size?","o":["About 1 µm, similar to a mitochondrion","About 1 nm, similar to an atom","About 10 nm, similar to a polio virus","About 100 µm, similar to an animal cell","About 1 mm, similar to a frog egg"],"e":"Bacteria are about 1 µm, similar in size to our mitochondria (and each of our cells contains thousands of mitochondria). Animal cells are about 10–100 µm, smallpox and polio viruses lie towards 10 nm or smaller, and a frog egg is about 1 mm.","w":["Atoms are around 0.1 nm.","Viruses like polio are at the tens-of-nm end.","That is an animal cell.","That is a frog egg."],"k":"Bacteria ≈ 1 µm ≈ mitochondrion.","rn":[["","How big is a typical bacterium compared with our cells?","How big is a typical bacterium compared with our cells?","About 1 micrometre; Similar to a mitochondrion; Animal cell: 10–100 micrometres"]]},{"id":34023,"t":"Types of Microorganisms","s":"Because microbes are so small, which feature is essential for their survival?","o":["Membrane transport of nutrients in and products out","A nucleus to protect DNA from the environment","Mitochondria to generate enough ATP","A multicellular body to share metabolic work","An endoskeleton to resist osmotic pressure"],"e":"Key microbial features are a boundary (a membrane, with a cell wall in some), a cytoplasm (aqueous mixture of macromolecules and organelles in some) and transport: nutrients in and products out, relying on membrane permeability and transport mechanisms. Bacteria lack a nucleus and mitochondria and are unicellular.","w":["Bacteria lack a nucleus.","Bacteria lack mitochondria.","Bacteria are unicellular.","Microbes do not have an endoskeleton."],"k":"Microbes depend on membrane transport of nutrients and products.","rn":[["","Why do microbes need membrane permeability and transport?","Why do microbes need membrane permeability and transport?","Nutrients in, products out"]]},{"id":34024,"t":"Taxonomy","s":"Which way of writing a bacterial name follows the rules of scientific naming?","o":["<i>Streptococcus pyogenes</i>, shortened to <i>S. pyogenes</i>","Streptococcus Pyogenes, shortened to Strep. Pyogenes","<i>pyogenes Streptococcus</i>, shortened to <i>p. Streptococcus</i>","STREPTOCOCCUS PYOGENES, shortened to S.P.","<i>Streptococcus</i> Pyogenes, shortened to <i>Strep</i>"],"e":"Scientific names have two parts, Genus + species, written in italics (or underlined), as in <i>Homo sapiens</i>. The genus has a capital letter and the species is lower case; the genus can be shortened to its first letter, e.g. <i>S. aureus</i>, <i>S. pyogenes</i>, <i>E. coli</i>.","w":["Names should be italicised and the species is lower case.","Genus comes first, then species.","Capitals and initials-only are wrong.","The species is lower case and both parts are italicised."],"k":"Italic Genus species; genus abbreviated to its initial.","rn":[["","Give 3 scientific bacterial names written in full.","Give 3 scientific bacterial names written in full.","Staphylococcus aureus; Streptococcus pyogenes; Escherichia coli"],["","Name the Lancefield streptococcus groups","Name the Lancefield streptococcus groups","A (S. pyogenes); B, C and G; D (= enterococcus)"]]},{"id":34025,"t":"Taxonomy","s":"A child has bloody diarrhoea followed by acute kidney injury after eating undercooked burgers; <i>E. coli</i> O157:H7 is isolated. What does this strain designation tell you?","o":["A strain defined by its O antigen, linked to HUS","It names a new genus of bacteria related to <i>Escherichia</i>","It means the organism is resistant to 157 antibiotics","It describes the shape and Gram reaction of the bacterium","It shows the organism is a commensal that never causes disease"],"e":"Within any species there are many strains. In <i>E. coli</i> O157:H7, “O157” is the O antigen, and H7 is linked on the slide to haemolytic uraemic syndrome: damage to small blood vessels in the kidney, which can cause acute renal failure and long-term damage. Strain naming matters clinically because some strains cause serious disease.","w":["<i>Escherichia</i> is the genus; O157:H7 is a strain.","The numbers are antigen designations, not resistance counts.","Shape and Gram stain are not given by the strain name.","This strain causes serious disease."],"k":"O157:H7 = E. coli strain (O antigen) linked to HUS.","rn":[["","What does E. coli O157:H7 tell you?","What does E. coli O157:H7 tell you?","E. coli strain; With O157 antigen; Linked to haemolytic uraemic syndrome"]]},{"id":34026,"t":"Taxonomy","s":"Which common name–scientific name pair is correct?","o":["Meningococcus = <i>Neisseria meningitidis</i>","Pneumococcus = <i>Neisseria meningitidis</i>","Meningococcus = <i>Streptococcus pneumoniae</i>","Pneumococcus = <i>Haemophilus influenzae</i>","Meningococcus = <i>Haemophilus influenzae</i>"],"e":"Common English names can be used: pneumococcus = <i>Streptococcus pneumoniae</i> and meningococcus = <i>Neisseria meningitidis</i>. <i>Haemophilus influenzae</i> is a Gram-negative rod whose capsule confers virulence.","w":["Pneumococcus is <i>S. pneumoniae</i>.","The pairs are swapped.","<i>H. influenzae</i> is not pneumococcus.","<i>H. influenzae</i> is not meningococcus."],"k":"Pneumococcus = S. pneumoniae; meningococcus = N. meningitidis.","rn":[["","Meningococcus is which species?","Meningococcus is which species?","Neisseria meningitidis"]]},{"id":34027,"t":"Bacterial Classification","s":"Why is it clinically important to classify bacteria?","o":["Bacteria differ in disease, diagnosis and treatment","All bacteria cause the same disease but at different speeds","Classification determines whether a patient is infectious to others","Only classified bacteria can be grown in the laboratory","Classification is required before Koch's postulates can be applied"],"e":"Only a few bacteria are medically important, and they differ clinically, diagnostically and in treatment. You cannot treat all bacterial infections with one type of antibiotic, so classification matters. For clinical purposes phenotypic data (shape, staining, growth) are most useful and rest on an understanding of bacterial structure and biology.","w":["Different bacteria cause different diseases.","Infectivity is not what classification determines.","Growth does not depend on classification.","Koch's postulates do not require prior classification."],"k":"Classification guides diagnosis and treatment choice.","rn":[["","What are the 3 ways medically important bacteria differ?","What are the 3 ways medically important bacteria differ?","Clinically; Diagnostically; In terms of treatment"]]},{"id":34028,"t":"Bacterial Classification","s":"For clinical purposes, which type of data is of most practical value in classifying bacteria?","o":["Phenotypic data, such as shape, staining and growth","Genotypic data from whole-genome sequencing alone, without any culture","The geographic origin of the patient","The patient's antibody titres","The number of plasmids in the bacterium"],"e":"Classification uses phenotypic and genotypic data, but clinically the phenotypic data (morphology, Gram staining, growth characteristics) are of most practical value and rest on knowledge of bacterial structure and biology. Molecular methods and biochemical tests can then identify specific strains.","w":["Genotypic data are used but are not the main practical clinical tool.","Geography does not classify bacteria.","Antibody titres are host data.","Plasmid number is not a classification criterion."],"k":"Clinical classification relies mainly on phenotype.","rn":[["","What data classify bacteria?","What data classify bacteria?","Phenotypic and genotypic; Phenotypic is most useful clinically"]]},{"id":34029,"t":"Bacterial Classification","s":"A Gram stain shows comma-shaped organisms in a patient with profuse watery diarrhoea. What is the term for this shape?","a":["vibrio","vibrios","comma shaped","comma-shaped"],"e":"Comma-shaped bacteria are vibrios (e.g. <i>Vibrio cholerae</i>, a Gram-negative rod on the medically important list). Other shapes: coccus (round), bacillus (rod), coccobacillus (between the two), spirillum and spirochete (spiral, depending on the degree of twisting).","k":"Comma-shaped bacterium = vibrio.","rn":[["","What are comma-shaped bacteria called?","What are comma-shaped bacteria called?","Vibrio"]]},{"id":34030,"t":"Bacterial Classification","s":"An organism is neither clearly round nor clearly rod-shaped but somewhere in between. Which term describes it?","o":["Coccobacillus","Spirochete","Vibrio","Spirillum","Diplococcus"],"e":"Bacteria between cocci and bacilli (not very long, not very round) are coccobacilli. Spiral bacteria are spirilla or spirochetes depending on how tightly twisted they are; comma-shaped ones are vibrios. Most medically important bacteria are cocci or bacilli.","w":["Spirochetes are tightly twisted spirals.","Vibrios are comma-shaped.","Spirilla are spiral.","A diplococcus is a pair of round cells, not an intermediate shape."],"k":"Coccobacillus = intermediate between coccus and bacillus."},{"id":34031,"t":"Bacterial Classification","s":"During a Gram stain, a trainee forgets the safranin step. What will they see under the microscope?","o":["Gram-positive organisms purple, Gram-negative organisms almost invisible","All organisms pink, so none can be classified","Gram-negative organisms purple and Gram-positive organisms colourless","All organisms purple regardless of their wall","No organisms, because they wash off without safranin"],"e":"Crystal violet set with iodine stains all cells; alcohol/acetone then decolourises Gram-negatives (thin peptidoglycan) while Gram-positives (thick peptidoglycan) keep the dye. Safranin is the pink counterstain that makes the decolourised Gram-negatives visible; without it you could not see their morphology.","w":["Pink comes from safranin, which was omitted.","Gram-negatives lose the violet; Gram-positives keep it.","Decolourisation removes violet from Gram-negatives.","Fixation keeps cells on the slide; safranin is only for visibility."],"k":"Safranin counterstain reveals decolourised Gram-negatives.","rn":[["","Gram positive vs Gram negative: colour?","Gram positive vs Gram negative: colour?","Positive: purple; Negative: pink; MNEMONIC"],["","Gram positive vs Gram negative: peptidoglycan?","Gram positive vs Gram negative: peptidoglycan?","Positive: thick; Negative: thin"]]},{"id":34032,"t":"Bacterial Classification","s":"Put the Gram stain in order. Which sequence is correct?","o":["Fix → crystal violet + iodine → alcohol/acetone → safranin","Fix → safranin → alcohol/acetone → crystal violet + iodine","Crystal violet → safranin → iodine → alcohol/acetone","Fix → alcohol/acetone → crystal violet + iodine → safranin","Iodine → crystal violet → safranin → alcohol/acetone"],"e":"Dry on a glass slide (fixation); stain with crystal violet and set with iodine; decolourise with alcohol/acetone; counterstain with safranin (pink). Positive = purple (thick peptidoglycan retains the violet); negative = pink (thin peptidoglycan loses it, then takes up safranin).","w":["Safranin is the final counterstain.","Iodine sets the violet and decolourisation precedes safranin.","Decolourising before staining would have nothing to remove.","Crystal violet comes before iodine, and safranin is last."],"k":"Gram stain order: fix, violet+iodine, decolourise, safranin.","rn":[["","Gram staining: last 2 steps?","Gram staining: last 2 steps?","Decolourise (alcohol/acetone); Counterstain with safranin"],["","Gram staining: first 2 steps?","Gram staining: first 2 steps?","Dry on glass plate; Crystal violet, set with iodine"]]},{"id":34033,"t":"Bacterial Envelope and Endotoxin","s":"What is the main structural basis for an organism staining Gram positive?","o":["A thick, accessible peptidoglycan layer that retains crystal violet","An outer membrane rich in lipopolysaccharide that binds and holds the iodine","A polysaccharide capsule that absorbs safranin","A mycolic acid layer that resists decolourisation","A periplasmic space filled with teichoic acid only"],"e":"The ability to take up and keep the stain depends on the thickness and accessibility of the cell-wall peptidoglycan. Gram-positives have a uniformly dense, thick peptidoglycan wall (with teichoic and lipoteichoic acids) that holds the crystal violet; Gram-negatives have thin peptidoglycan and an outer membrane with LPS and lose it.","w":["An LPS outer membrane is a Gram-negative feature.","The capsule is not the basis of the Gram reaction.","Mycolic acid makes mycobacteria stain poorly with Gram's stain.","Teichoic acids are present but the thick peptidoglycan is the key."],"k":"Gram positive = thick peptidoglycan retains violet.","rn":[["","What 2 components are in the Gram positive envelope?","What 2 components are in the Gram positive envelope?","Thick peptidoglycan; Lipoteichoic and teichoic acid"],["","Gram positive vs Gram negative: peptidoglycan?","Gram positive vs Gram negative: peptidoglycan?","Positive: thick; Negative: thin"],["","What cell wall do mycobacteria have?","What cell wall do mycobacteria have?","Thick, Gram positive-like; Thick lipid layer; Mycolic acid mycomembrane"]]},{"id":34034,"t":"Bacterial Envelope and Endotoxin","s":"Which feature is found in Gram-negative but not Gram-positive bacteria?","o":["An outer membrane containing lipopolysaccharide","A thick peptidoglycan wall with lipoteichoic acid","Lipoteichoic acid","A plasma membrane","70S ribosomes"],"e":"Gram-negatives have a thin peptidoglycan layer plus an outer membrane with lipopolysaccharide (endotoxin), proteins and porins, and an inner membrane. Gram-positives have thick peptidoglycan with teichoic and lipoteichoic acid and no outer membrane. Both have a plasma membrane and 70S ribosomes.","w":["Thick peptidoglycan with lipoteichoic acid is Gram-positive.","Lipoteichoic acid is Gram-positive.","Both have a plasma membrane.","Both have 70S ribosomes."],"k":"Outer membrane with LPS = Gram negative only.","rn":[["","Endotoxin","Endotoxin","Lipopolysaccharide; Of Gram negative outer membrane"],["","Gram negative envelope: 3 outer parts?","Gram negative envelope: 3 outer parts?","Outer membrane; Lipopolysaccharide; Proteins and pores"]]},{"id":34035,"t":"Bacterial Classification","s":"Which organisms lack a cell wall and so cannot be classified by Gram staining?","o":["<i>Mycoplasma</i> and <i>Chlamydia</i>","<i>Staphylococcus</i> and <i>Streptococcus</i>","<i>Neisseria</i> and <i>Moraxella</i>","<i>Bacillus</i> and <i>Clostridium</i>","<i>Pseudomonas</i> and <i>Klebsiella</i>"],"e":"Some bacteria lack a cell wall and so are not “free-living”: <i>Mycoplasma</i> (e.g. <i>M. pneumoniae</i>, a very common cause of pneumonia) and <i>Chlamydia</i> (e.g. <i>C. trachomatis</i>). Mycobacteria have a wall that simply does not stain well with Gram's stain. The other pairs have walls and Gram-stain normally.","w":["These are Gram-positive cocci.","These are Gram-negative cocci.","These are spore-forming organisms with walls.","These are Gram-negative rods."],"k":"No cell wall: Mycoplasma, Chlamydia.","rn":[["","Which bacteria lack a cell wall?","Which bacteria lack a cell wall?","Mycoplasma; Chlamydia"]]},{"id":34036,"t":"Infection and Human Disease","s":"A blood culture grows Gram-positive cocci in clusters from a patient with an infected central line. Which genus is most likely?","o":["<i>Staphylococcus</i>","<i>Streptococcus</i>","<i>Neisseria</i>","Corynebacterium","<i>Haemophilus</i>"],"e":"Gram-positive cocci grow either in clusters (staphylococci) or chains (streptococci), a distinction especially important for this group. <i>S. aureus</i> (including MRSA) is a common nosocomial and implant/device-related pathogen. <i>Neisseria</i> are Gram-negative cocci, <i>Bacillus</i> Gram-positive rods and <i>Haemophilus</i> a Gram-negative rod.","w":["Streptococci form chains.","<i>Neisseria</i> are Gram-negative.","<i>Corynebacterium</i> are Gram-positive rods, not cocci.","<i>Haemophilus</i> is a Gram-negative rod."],"k":"Gram-positive cocci in clusters = staphylococci.","rn":[["","What is MRSA (methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus)?","What is MRSA (methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus)?","Staphylococcus aureus resistant to oxacillin"],["","What are the 3 key nosocomial organisms?","What are the 3 key nosocomial organisms?","Staphylococcus aureus (incl. MRSA); Enterococcus species (incl. VRE); Clostridium difficile"]]},{"id":34037,"t":"Bacterial Classification","s":"A Gram stain of a throat swab from a febrile child shows Gram-positive cocci in chains. Which organism on the medically important list fits?","o":["<i>Streptococcus pyogenes</i> (Group A)","<i>Staphylococcus aureus</i>","<i>Moraxella catarrhalis</i>","<i>Haemophilus influenzae</i>","<i>Bordetella pertussis</i>"],"e":"Streptococci grow in chains; Group A streptococcus is <i>S. pyogenes</i>. <i>S. aureus</i> grows in clusters. <i>Moraxella catarrhalis</i> is a Gram-negative coccus; <i>H. influenzae</i> and <i>B. pertussis</i> are Gram-negative rods.","w":["Staphylococci form clusters.","<i>Moraxella</i> is a Gram-negative coccus.","<i>Haemophilus</i> is a Gram-negative rod.","<i>Bordetella</i> is a Gram-negative rod."],"k":"Gram-positive cocci in chains = streptococci (e.g. S. pyogenes).","rn":[["","Name the Lancefield streptococcus groups","Name the Lancefield streptococcus groups","A (S. pyogenes); B, C and G; D (= enterococcus)"]]},{"id":34038,"t":"Bacterial Classification","s":"CSF from a teenager with a petechial rash and neck stiffness shows Gram-negative cocci. Which organism is most likely?","o":["<i>Neisseria meningitidis</i>","<i>Streptococcus pneumoniae</i>","<i>Haemophilus influenzae</i>","<i>Listeria</i>-type Gram-positive rods","<i>Staphylococcus aureus</i>"],"e":"The Gram-negative cocci on the list are <i>Neisseria</i> (<i>N. meningitidis</i>, the meningococcus; <i>N. gonorrhoea</i>) and <i>Moraxella catarrhalis</i>. <i>S. pneumoniae</i> and <i>S. aureus</i> are Gram-positive cocci; <i>H. influenzae</i> is a Gram-negative rod.","w":["Pneumococcus is a Gram-positive coccus.","<i>H. influenzae</i> is a rod.","Rods are not cocci and these would be Gram positive.","<i>S. aureus</i> is Gram positive."],"k":"Gram-negative cocci: Neisseria, Moraxella.","rn":[["","Which Gram negative cocci are there?","Which Gram negative cocci are there?","Neisseria (N. meningitidis, N. gonorrhoea); Moraxella (Branhamella) catarrhalis"]]},{"id":34039,"t":"Bacterial Classification","s":"Which organism is a strict anaerobe on the medically important bacteria list?","o":["<i>Bacteroides fragilis</i>","<i>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</i>","<i>Escherichia coli</i>","<i>Legionella pneumophila</i>","<i>Haemophilus influenzae</i>"],"e":"Strict anaerobes need an environment fully depleted of oxygen, which can be toxic to them. On the list these are <i>Bacteroides</i> (e.g. <i>B. fragilis</i>, a Gram-negative rod) and <i>Peptococcus/Peptostreptococcus</i> (Gram-positive cocci). The other options are Gram-negative rods that are not strict anaerobes.","w":["<i>Pseudomonas</i> is not listed as a strict anaerobe.","<i>E. coli</i> is not a strict anaerobe.","<i>Legionella</i> is not listed as a strict anaerobe.","<i>Haemophilus</i> is not listed as a strict anaerobe."],"k":"Strict anaerobes: Bacteroides, Peptococcus/Peptostreptococcus.","rn":[["","What are 2 examples of strict anaerobes?","What are 2 examples of strict anaerobes?","Peptococcus / Peptostreptococcus; Bacteroides fragilis"]]},{"id":34040,"t":"Bacterial Classification","s":"Which organism is correctly classified?","o":["<i>Bacillus anthracis</i>: Gram-positive rod","<i>Neisseria gonorrhoea</i>: Gram-positive coccus","<i>Vibrio cholerae</i>: Gram-positive rod","<i>Enterococcus</i>: Gram-negative rod","<i>Bordetella pertussis</i>: Gram-negative coccus"],"e":"<i>Bacillus</i> (<i>B. cereus</i>, <i>B. anthracis</i>) are Gram-positive rods. <i>Neisseria</i> are Gram-negative cocci; <i>Vibrio cholerae</i> and <i>Bordetella pertussis</i> are Gram-negative rods; enterococcus (Group D streptococcus) is a Gram-positive coccus.","w":["<i>Neisseria</i> are Gram-negative.","<i>V. cholerae</i> is Gram-negative.","Enterococci are Gram-positive cocci.","<i>B. pertussis</i> is a rod."],"k":"Bacillus spp. = Gram-positive rods.","rn":[["","Name the Gram positive rods.","Name the Gram positive rods.","Bacillus (B. cereus, B. anthracis); Corynebacterium"]]},{"id":34041,"t":"Bacterial Classification","s":"Which of these is NOT a Gram-negative rod?","o":["<i>Corynebacterium</i>","<i>Klebsiella</i>","<i>Campylobacter</i>","<i>Proteus</i>","<i>Legionella pneumophila</i>"],"e":"<i>Corynebacterium</i> is listed with <i>Bacillus</i> among the Gram-positive rods. Gram-negative rods include <i>Haemophilus</i>, the Enterobacteriaceae (<i>Salmonella</i>, <i>Shigella</i>, <i>E. coli</i>, <i>Klebsiella</i>, <i>Enterobacter</i>, <i>Proteus</i>), <i>Pseudomonas</i>, <i>Bacteroides</i>, <i>Campylobacter</i>, <i>Helicobacter</i>, <i>Vibrio</i>, <i>Bordetella</i> and <i>Legionella</i>.","w":["<i>Klebsiella</i> is a Gram-negative rod (Enterobacteriaceae).","<i>Campylobacter</i> is a Gram-negative rod.","<i>Proteus</i> is a Gram-negative rod.","<i>Legionella</i> is a Gram-negative rod."],"k":"Corynebacterium = Gram-positive rod.","rn":[["","Name the Gram positive rods.","Name the Gram positive rods.","Bacillus (B. cereus, B. anthracis); Corynebacterium"]]},{"id":34042,"t":"Bacterial Classification","s":"Enterococci, a cause of nosocomial infection (including VRE), belong to which streptococcal group?","a":["group d","d","group d streptococcus","lancefield group d"],"e":"On the medically important list, Group D streptococcus = enterococcus. Other streptococci: Group A (<i>S. pyogenes</i>), Group B, Groups C and G, <i>S. pneumoniae</i> and viridans-type streptococci. “Entero” means gut.","k":"Enterococcus = Group D streptococcus.","rn":[["","Group D streptococcus is also called what?","Group D streptococcus is also called what?","Enterococcus"],["","Name the Lancefield streptococcus groups","Name the Lancefield streptococcus groups","A (S. pyogenes); B, C and G; D (= enterococcus)"]]},{"id":34043,"t":"Peptidoglycan and Mycobacteria","s":"Which description of peptidoglycan is correct?","o":["A 3D NAG/NAM sugar polymer with short peptides, cross-linked by transpeptidases","A linear chain of glucose units joined by peptide bonds, synthesised by human cells","A lipid bilayer containing lipid A and O antigen","A protein coat made of capsomeres around nucleic acid","A polysaccharide capsule that hides the immunogenic cell wall"],"e":"Peptidoglycan (“peptido-glycan”) is a three-dimensional polymer of N-acetylated sugars, N-acetylglucosamine (NAG) and N-acetylmuramic acid (NAM), with 3–5 amino-acid peptides made of amino acids peculiar to peptidoglycan and resistant to enzymatic destruction, cross-linked by transpeptidase enzymes.","w":["It is not made by human cells and is a cross-linked 3D polymer.","That describes LPS of the outer membrane.","That is a viral capsid.","That is the capsule."],"k":"Peptidoglycan = NAG/NAM + short peptides, transpeptidase cross-links.","rn":[["","What are the 3 components of peptidoglycan?","What are the 3 components of peptidoglycan?","N-acetylated sugars (NAG, NAM); 3-5 amino acid peptides; Cross-links by transpeptidase"]]},{"id":34044,"t":"Peptidoglycan and Mycobacteria","s":"Name the enzyme family that cross-links the peptide side-chains of peptidoglycan, the final step targeted by an important class of antibiotics.","a":["transpeptidase","transpeptidases","transpeptidase enzymes"],"e":"Peptidoglycan synthesis has three targetable steps: polymerisation of sugars (to make the backbone), elongation of amino-acid side chains (to add the peptides) and transpeptidation (to cross-link). Blocking any step gives a faulty wall that collapses, killing the bacterium.","k":"Transpeptidase cross-links peptidoglycan.","rn":[["","What are the 3 steps of peptidoglycan synthesis?","What are the 3 steps of peptidoglycan synthesis?","Sugars polymerise (backbone); Peptide side-chains elongate; Transpeptidase cross-links"]]},{"id":34045,"t":"Peptidoglycan and Mycobacteria","s":"Why are antibiotics that block peptidoglycan synthesis relatively safe for human cells?","o":["The pathway is unique to bacteria","Human cells repair their peptidoglycan faster than bacteria","Human peptidoglycan is shielded by the plasma membrane","The drugs cannot enter human tissues","Human transpeptidases are resistant to all antibiotics"],"e":"Peptidoglycan synthetic pathways are unique to bacteria, and all steps (sugar polymerisation, side-chain elongation, transpeptidase cross-linking) are targeted by important antibiotic classes. A faulty layer collapses, the contents leak and the bacterium dies, without affecting human cells, which make no peptidoglycan.","w":["Human cells do not make peptidoglycan.","There is no human peptidoglycan to shield.","Selectivity comes from target absence, not exclusion from tissues.","Humans lack peptidoglycan transpeptidases."],"k":"Peptidoglycan pathway is bacteria-only → selective target.","rn":[["","Why is peptidoglycan synthesis a good antibiotic target?","Why is peptidoglycan synthesis a good antibiotic target?","Unique to bacteria; Does not affect our cells"]]},{"id":34046,"t":"Peptidoglycan and Mycobacteria","s":"An antibiotic makes bacteria build a weakened, poorly cross-linked peptidoglycan. What is the most likely result for the bacterium?","o":["The wall collapses and the contents escape","The bacterium switches to fermentation and keeps growing","The bacterium forms a capsule that replaces the wall","The bacterium becomes Gram negative and survives","Only the ribosomes are affected, slowing protein synthesis"],"e":"Peptidoglycan protects the bacterium from its environment and gives structural integrity. A faulty layer can collapse, letting the inside of the bacterium come out, so the bacterium dies. The capsule is a separate polysaccharide coat and does not substitute for the wall.","w":["Metabolic switching does not restore the wall.","The capsule cannot replace peptidoglycan.","Gram status depends on wall structure, but this does not rescue the cell.","Ribosomes are not the target here."],"k":"Faulty peptidoglycan → collapse and lysis.","rn":[["","What happens when antibiotics block peptidoglycan synthesis?","What happens when antibiotics block peptidoglycan synthesis?","Faulty wall collapses; Contents leak, bacterium dies"]]},{"id":34047,"t":"Peptidoglycan and Mycobacteria","s":"Sputum from a man with a three-month cough, night sweats and weight loss shows no organisms on Gram stain, yet tuberculosis is suspected. Why might the Gram stain be falsely negative?","o":["The waxy mycolic acid layer stops the stain entering the wall","The organism has no cell wall at all, like <i>Mycoplasma</i> and <i>Chlamydia</i>","The organism is too large to be seen with a light microscope","Tuberculosis is caused by a virus","The thick capsule absorbs the safranin"],"e":"Mycobacteria have basically a Gram-positive-type wall, but a very thick lipid (mycolic acid) mycomembrane anchored to the peptidoglycan. This waxy coat does not take up Gram's stain, giving a false negative, but is acid fast, so an acid-fast stain is used when <i>M. tuberculosis</i> is suspected. The lipid coat also confers virulence and is anti-phagocytic.","w":["Mycobacteria have a wall; <i>Mycoplasma</i> lack one.","Bacteria are about 1 µm and visible with a light microscope.","TB is caused by <i>M. tuberculosis</i>, a bacterium.","The mycolic acid layer, not a capsule, is the reason."],"k":"Mycolic acid → poor Gram staining; use acid-fast stain.","rn":[["","What is the mycolic acid layer like?","What is the mycolic acid layer like?","Waxy; acid fast; Anti-phagocytic; confers virulence"],["","Why does the mycomembrane not take up Gram stain?","Why does the mycomembrane not take up Gram stain?","It is waxy (mycolic acid)"],["","What cell wall do mycobacteria have?","What cell wall do mycobacteria have?","Thick, Gram positive-like; Thick lipid layer; Mycolic acid mycomembrane"]]},{"id":34048,"t":"Peptidoglycan and Mycobacteria","s":"Which stain is used to identify <i>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</i> instead of the Gram stain?","a":["acid fast","acid-fast","acid fast stain","acid-fast stain","ziehl-neelsen","ziehl neelsen","zn"],"e":"The waxy mycolic acid layer of mycobacteria does not Gram stain but is acid fast, so an acid-fast dye is used when <i>M. tuberculosis</i> is suspected. The same lipid coat supports intracellular survival and makes the organism anti-phagocytic.","k":"Mycobacteria: acid-fast stain.","rn":[["","Which stain is used for mycobacteria?","Which stain is used for mycobacteria?","Acid fast stain"]]},{"id":34049,"t":"Peptidoglycan and Mycobacteria","s":"How does the mycolic acid mycomembrane contribute to the virulence of <i>M. tuberculosis</i>?","o":["It is anti-phagocytic and helps intracellular survival","It releases lipid A, causing septic shock","It encodes transposase to spread resistance genes","It allows the organism to form spores in the lung","It makes the organism grow rapidly in exponential phase"],"e":"The very thick lipid (mycolic acid) membrane anchored to the peptidoglycan protects the intracellular contents and aids intracellular survival; the lipid coat confers virulence and is anti-phagocytic. Lipid A is part of Gram-negative LPS; spores are made by <i>Bacillus</i> and <i>Clostridium</i>.","w":["Lipid A is a Gram-negative LPS component.","Transposase is encoded by transposons.","Mycobacteria are not the spore-formers named.","The coat does not speed growth."],"k":"Mycolic acid: anti-phagocytic, intracellular survival.","rn":[["","What does the mycolic acid layer give mycobacteria?","What does the mycolic acid layer give mycobacteria?","Intracellular survival; Virulence; Anti-phagocytic"],["","What does the mycolic acid mycomembrane do for mycobacteria?","What does the mycolic acid mycomembrane do for mycobacteria?","Anchored to peptidoglycan; Helps intracellular survival"]]},{"id":34050,"t":"Peptidoglycan and Mycobacteria","s":"Which organism is NOT a member of the <i>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</i> complex?","o":["<i>M. leprae</i>","<i>M. bovis</i>","<i>M. africanum</i>","<i>M. microti</i>","<i>M. tuberculosis</i>"],"e":"The <i>M. tuberculosis</i> complex (causes of TB) is <i>M. tuberculosis</i>, <i>M. bovis</i>, <i>M. africanum</i> and <i>M. microti</i>. <i>M. leprae</i> causes leprosy. “Atypical” or non-tuberculous mycobacteria include <i>M. ulcerans</i>, <i>M. avium-intracellulare</i>, <i>M. kansasii</i> and <i>M. chelonae</i>.","w":["<i>M. bovis</i> is in the TB complex.","<i>M. africanum</i> is in the TB complex.","<i>M. microti</i> is in the TB complex.","<i>M. tuberculosis</i> is the core member."],"k":"M. leprae causes leprosy, not part of the TB complex.","rn":[["","Which mycobacteria cause leprosy and TB?","Which mycobacteria cause leprosy and TB?","Leprosy: M. leprae; TB: M. tuberculosis complex"],["","Which mycobacterium causes leprosy?","Which mycobacterium causes leprosy?","M. leprae"]]},{"id":34051,"t":"Peptidoglycan and Mycobacteria","s":"<i>M. avium-intracellulare</i> and <i>M. kansasii</i> belong to which group of mycobacteria?","o":["Atypical (non-tuberculous) mycobacteria","The <i>M. tuberculosis</i> complex","Causes of leprosy","Wall-less <i>Mycoplasma</i> species","Gram-negative acid-fast cocci"],"e":"“Atypical” mycobacteria are the non-tuberculous mycobacteria, e.g. <i>M. ulcerans</i>, <i>M. avium-intracellulare</i>, <i>M. kansasii</i> and <i>M. chelonae</i>. They differ from the TB complex (<i>M. tuberculosis</i>, <i>bovis</i>, <i>africanum</i>, <i>microti</i>) and from <i>M. leprae</i>.","w":["These are not TB-complex members.","Leprosy is caused by <i>M. leprae</i>.","<i>Mycoplasma</i> is a different, wall-less genus.","Mycobacteria have a Gram-positive-type wall and are not cocci."],"k":"MAI, M. kansasii = atypical mycobacteria.","rn":[["","Non-tuberculous mycobacteria are also called what?","Non-tuberculous mycobacteria are also called what?","\"Atypical\" mycobacteria"]]},{"id":34052,"t":"Bacterial Growth","s":"Why does it matter whether Gram-positive cocci grow in clusters or chains?","o":["It separates staphylococci from streptococci","It shows whether the organism is aerobic or anaerobic","It determines whether the organism has an outer membrane","It reveals whether the organism carries plasmids","It shows the phase of the growth curve"],"e":"Bacteria may grow in clusters or chains, which is especially important for Gram-positive cocci: staphylococci form clusters and streptococci chains. This microscopic pattern, along with colony characteristics on solid media and excreted enzymes and waste products, can be exploited for diagnosis. Cells in the middle of the group also gain simple protection.","w":["Atmosphere is a separate growth requirement.","Outer membranes relate to Gram-negative status.","Plasmid carriage is not seen this way.","Arrangement is not a growth-phase marker."],"k":"Clusters = staph, chains = strep.","rn":[["","Staphylococci vs streptococci: growth pattern?","Staphylococci vs streptococci: growth pattern?","Staphylococci: clusters; Streptococci: chains"]]},{"id":34053,"t":"Bacterial Growth","s":"An organism uses oxygen as its final electron acceptor, oxidising glucose fully to CO₂ and H₂O. It is best described as:","o":["an aerobe","an obligate anaerobe","a facultative anaerobe that cannot use oxygen","a fermenter producing lactic acid","a spore in its dormant phase"],"e":"Aerobes use O₂ as the final electron acceptor, which is very efficient (e.g. glucose oxidised to CO₂ and H₂O). Obligate anaerobes ferment, using an organic molecule as the final acceptor (e.g. glucose → lactic acid), and oxygen is usually toxic to them. Facultative anaerobes can switch between the two.","w":["Obligate anaerobes cannot use oxygen.","Facultative anaerobes can use oxygen.","Fermentation does not produce CO₂ and H₂O this way.","Spores are metabolically inactive."],"k":"Aerobe: O₂ is the final electron acceptor."},{"id":34054,"t":"Bacterial Growth","s":"A wound swab grows an organism only in an oxygen-free jar, and the culture accumulates lactic acid. What type of organism is this?","o":["An obligate anaerobe using fermentation","An aerobe using oxidative metabolism","A facultative anaerobe that needs oxygen","A strict aerobe poisoned by lactic acid","An organism that cannot grow in any culture"],"e":"Obligate anaerobes use fermentation with an organic molecule as the final electron acceptor (e.g. glucose to lactic acid); this is OK when substrates are plentiful, and oxygen is usually toxic to them. The waste product (lactic acid) can help identify them. Facultative anaerobes could also grow with oxygen.","w":["Aerobes need oxygen.","Facultative anaerobes do not need oxygen; they can switch.","It grew without oxygen, so it is not an aerobe.","It grew in the anaerobic jar."],"k":"Grows only without O₂, makes lactic acid = obligate anaerobe."},{"id":34055,"t":"Bacterial Growth","s":"An organism grows equally well in aerobic and anaerobic incubators. Name this type of organism.","a":["facultative anaerobe","facultative anaerobes","facultative"],"e":"Facultative anaerobes can switch between aerobic metabolism (O₂ as final electron acceptor) and anaerobic metabolism (fermentation). Atmospheric requirements are one of the growth properties exploited to diagnose infection.","k":"Can switch aerobic/anaerobic = facultative anaerobe.","rn":[["","What is a facultative anaerobe?","What is a facultative anaerobe?","Switches between; Aerobic and anaerobic"]]},{"id":34056,"t":"Bacterial Growth","s":"Why is <i>E. coli</i> easy to grow in the laboratory but <i>Treponema pallidum</i> very difficult?","o":["<i>E. coli</i> needs only glucose and salts; <i>T. pallidum</i> needs enriched medium","<i>E. coli</i> is an obligate anaerobe; <i>T. pallidum</i> is an aerobe","<i>E. coli</i> is an obligate anaerobe whereas <i>T. pallidum</i> is a strict aerobe","<i>T. pallidum</i> lacks ribosomes and cannot make protein","<i>T. pallidum</i> is a virus and needs living cells"],"e":"Bacteria must import everything they cannot make: purines, pyrimidines, amino acids, vitamins. <i>E. coli</i> needs only glucose and inorganic salts, grows to high numbers and is widely used in biotechnology. <i>T. pallidum</i>, the cause of syphilis, needs a specialised enriched medium and is difficult to grow. Nutritional differences help identify bacteria.","w":["Atmosphere is not the explanation given.","Atmosphere is not the explanation given.","All bacteria have 70S ribosomes.","<i>T. pallidum</i> is a bacterium."],"k":"E. coli: glucose + salts; T. pallidum: enriched medium.","rn":[["","Easy vs hard to grow: example","Easy vs hard to grow: example","Easy: E. coli (glucose, salts); Hard: T. pallidum (enriched)"],["","Why is Treponema pallidum hard to grow?","Why is Treponema pallidum hard to grow?","Needs enriched medium"],["","What does E. coli need to grow?","What does E. coli need to grow?","Glucose and inorganic salts only"]]},{"id":34057,"t":"Bacterial Growth","s":"Which set of physical conditions is used in the laboratory to select for particular bacteria?","o":["Temperature, pH and salt content","Light intensity, humidity and air pressure only","Antibody titre, cytokine level and pH","Gram reaction, shape and capsule","Number of plasmids and transposons"],"e":"Physical growth requirements are temperature, pH and salt content, all used for selection in the lab. Most medically important bacteria are grown at 37 °C, some needing slight deviations, and selection leads to identification.","w":["These are not the listed physical requirements.","Antibodies and cytokines are host factors.","These are morphology/staining features.","These are genetic elements."],"k":"Lab selection uses temperature, pH, salt.","rn":[["","What are the 3 physical growth requirements for bacteria?","What are the 3 physical growth requirements for bacteria?","Temperature; pH; Salt content"]]},{"id":34058,"t":"Bacterial Growth","s":"Bacteria are transferred to fresh broth. For the first hour cell numbers do not rise while the bacteria switch on the genes needed for growth. Which phase is this?","o":["Lag phase","Exponential phase","Stationary phase","Death phase","Spore phase"],"e":"In the lag phase there is no increase in cell numbers while bacteria adjust to the new environment by gene regulation, switching growth and reproduction genes on once conditions are favourable. Exponential (log) phase follows with doubling by binary fission; stationary phase is a plateau as nutrients deplete; death phase follows exhaustion and toxicity.","w":["Numbers would be doubling.","Stationary phase follows growth as nutrients deplete.","Death follows exhaustion of resources.","“Spore phase” is not a growth-curve phase."],"k":"Lag phase: no increase, adjustment, gene regulation.","rn":[["","What happens in the lag phase?","What happens in the lag phase?","No increase in cell numbers; Adjusting to new environment; Gene regulation"],["","Why no growth in the lag phase?","Why no growth in the lag phase?","Switching on growth genes; Adjusting to environment"]]},{"id":34059,"t":"Bacterial Growth","s":"On a log plot of cell number against time, what does the slope of the exponential phase represent?","o":["The organism's growth rate there","The rate of spore formation","The rate at which nutrients are added","The toxicity of waste products","The number of plasmids per cell"],"e":"In the exponential (log) phase cells double by binary fission; the slope equals the growth rate of the organism in that environment. The rate is expressed as the generation (doubling) time, G = t/n, which also gives identifying information.","w":["Spores are not counted by the slope.","Nutrients are not being added.","Toxicity matters in the death phase.","Plasmid number is unrelated."],"k":"Exponential slope = growth rate.","rn":[["","What is the slope of the exponential phase?","What is the slope of the exponential phase?","Growth rate; In that environment"]]},{"id":34060,"t":"Bacterial Growth","s":"A culture runs for 120 minutes and passes through 6 generations in exponential phase. What is the generation time (in minutes)?","a":["20","20 minutes","20 min","20 mins"],"e":"Generation time G = t/n, the time per generation, which equals the population's doubling time. Here G = 120 min / 6 = 20 min. The exponential growth rate (slope of the log phase) depends on the organism and its environment.","k":"G = t/n.","rn":[["","What is generation time?","What is generation time?","Doubling time; Of the population"],["","What is the slope of the exponential phase?","What is the slope of the exponential phase?","Growth rate; In that environment"]]},{"id":34061,"t":"Bacterial Growth","s":"A broth culture stops increasing in cell number although cells are still alive; nutrients are depleted and metabolites have accumulated. Which phase is this, and what drives it?","o":["Stationary: depletion sensed, genes off, division stops","Lag; bacteria are still adjusting to fresh medium","Exponential; bacteria double by binary fission","Death; resources are fully exhausted and the medium is toxic","Lag; spores are germinating into vegetative cells"],"e":"In the stationary phase nutrients become depleted and metabolites build up; bacteria detect this, switch off some genes (gene regulation) and division stops, giving a plateau. If conditions do not change, resources are exhausted and the toxic environment leads to the death phase.","w":["Lag phase occurs at the start, before growth.","Exponential phase shows rising numbers.","Death phase shows falling numbers.","Spore germination is not the stationary phase."],"k":"Stationary: nutrients depleted, waste builds, division stops.","rn":[["","What happens to growth genes in the stationary phase?","What happens to growth genes in the stationary phase?","Gene regulation switches some off; Division stops"],["","What happens in the stationary phase?","What happens in the stationary phase?","Nutrients depleted; Metabolites build up; Division stops"]]},{"id":34062,"t":"Other Bacterial Features","s":"Which pairing of growth phase with clinical picture is described in the lecture?","o":["Stationary phase ≈ abscess","Lag phase ≈ abscess","Death phase ≈ bacteraemia","Stationary phase ≈ bacteraemia","Exponential phase ≈ abscess"],"e":"Bacteria regulate genes in response to their environment, including virulence factors. The exponential phase approximates superficial infection or bacteraemia (rapid multiplication), while the stationary phase approximates an abscess (nutrient-poor, waste-rich, little division).","w":["Lag phase is adjustment, not an abscess.","Death phase is not matched to bacteraemia.","Bacteraemia corresponds to exponential growth.","Exponential growth corresponds to superficial infection/bacteraemia."],"k":"Exponential ≈ bacteraemia/superficial; stationary ≈ abscess.","rn":[["","Exponential vs stationary phase: matching infection?","Exponential vs stationary phase: matching infection?","Exponential: superficial infection / bacteraemia; Stationary: abscess"]]},{"id":34063,"t":"Bacterial Growth","s":"Name the phase of the bacterial growth curve that follows the stationary phase when the environment does not change.","a":["death phase","death","decline phase","decline"],"e":"If the environment does not change, the stationary phase is followed by the death phase: resources are exhausted and the accumulated waste makes the environment toxic. The full sequence is lag → exponential → stationary → death.","k":"Lag → exponential → stationary → death.","rn":[["","What are the 4 phases of the bacterial growth curve?","What are the 4 phases of the bacterial growth curve?","Lag; Exponential (log); Stationary; Death"],["","What causes the death phase?","What causes the death phase?","Exhaustion of resources; Toxicity of environment"]]},{"id":34064,"t":"Bacterial Growth","s":"By what process do bacteria reproduce during the exponential phase?","o":["Binary fission","Budding","Spore formation","Meiosis","Conjugation"],"e":"Bacteria reproduce by binary fission (one cell splits into two), so numbers double each generation and the log-phase curve is steep. Budding is how yeasts such as <i>Candida</i> reproduce; spores are dormant forms; conjugation is horizontal gene transfer, not reproduction.","w":["Budding is yeast reproduction.","Spores are dormant, non-replicating forms.","Meiosis is a eukaryotic process.","Conjugation transfers genes between bacteria."],"k":"Bacteria divide by binary fission.","rn":[["","How do bacteria reproduce?","How do bacteria reproduce?","Binary fission"],["","What happens in the exponential phase?","What happens in the exponential phase?","Cell doubling (binary fission)"]]},{"id":34065,"t":"Bacterial Envelope and Endotoxin","s":"Which statement about the bacterial cell envelope is correct?","o":["It sets Gram reaction, antibiotic susceptibility and pathogenicity","It is identical in Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria","It contains mitochondria for energy production","It is absent from all medically important bacteria","It only matters for laboratory staining, not disease"],"e":"The envelope determines Gram staining, influences susceptibility to antibiotics and determines pathogenicity, for example lipopolysaccharide (endotoxin) occurs only in Gram negatives. Gram-positives: thick peptidoglycan with teichoic/lipoteichoic acid; Gram-negatives: thin peptidoglycan, outer membrane with LPS, porins and proteins.","w":["The two envelopes differ markedly.","Bacteria have no mitochondria.","Most have envelopes; only a few (e.g. <i>Mycoplasma</i>) lack a wall.","It also influences antibiotics and pathogenicity."],"k":"Envelope: Gram reaction, antibiotic susceptibility, pathogenicity.","rn":[["","What are the 3 things the bacterial envelope determines?","What are the 3 things the bacterial envelope determines?","Gram staining; Susceptibility to antibiotics; Pathogenicity"]]},{"id":34066,"t":"Bacterial Envelope and Endotoxin","s":"In a Gram-negative envelope, which structure lies between the outer membrane and the plasma (inner) membrane?","o":["The periplasmic space containing a thin peptidoglycan layer","The capsule containing teichoic acid","The nucleoid containing plasmids","A thick peptidoglycan layer studded with lipoteichoic acid","The mycolic acid mycomembrane"],"e":"Gram-negatives have, from outside in: outer membrane (with LPS, porins and proteins), the periplasmic space with a thin peptidoglycan layer, and the plasma membrane. Thick peptidoglycan with lipoteichoic acid is Gram-positive; mycolic acid belongs to mycobacteria.","w":["The capsule is outermost and is polysaccharide.","The nucleoid is in the cytoplasm.","Thick peptidoglycan with lipoteichoic acid is Gram-positive.","Mycolic acid is in mycobacteria."],"k":"Gram-negative: outer membrane → periplasm with thin peptidoglycan → inner membrane.","rn":[["","Gram negative envelope: 2 inner parts?","Gram negative envelope: 2 inner parts?","Thin peptidoglycan; Inner membrane"]]},{"id":34067,"t":"Bacterial Envelope and Endotoxin","s":"Name the channel-forming proteins in the Gram-negative outer membrane.","a":["porins","porin","pore proteins","pore protein"],"e":"The Gram-negative outer membrane contains lipopolysaccharide, porins (pore proteins) and other proteins, and is anchored to the thin peptidoglycan by lipoprotein. Gram-positives lack an outer membrane.","k":"Outer membrane pores = porins."},{"id":34068,"t":"Bacterial Envelope and Endotoxin","s":"Which statement best distinguishes endotoxin from exotoxin?","o":["Endotoxin is part of Gram-negative walls; exotoxins are exported by both","Endotoxin is secreted by Gram positives; exotoxins remain in the Gram-negative wall","Both are lipopolysaccharides but exotoxins lack lipid A","Endotoxins are proteins like botulinum toxin; exotoxins are LPS","Endotoxin is made by viruses; exotoxin by bacteria"],"e":"“Endo” means part of the bacterium: endotoxin is lipopolysaccharide (lipid A) in the outer membrane of Gram-negatives only; it is not released as a secreted product. “Exo” means comes out of: exotoxins, such as botulinum toxin, are made and exported, by both Gram-positives and Gram-negatives.","w":["This reverses the definitions.","Exotoxins are not LPS.","Botulinum toxin is an exotoxin; endotoxin is LPS.","Both are bacterial in this lecture."],"k":"Endotoxin = LPS, part of Gram-negatives; exotoxin = exported.","rn":[["","Endotoxin","Endotoxin","Lipopolysaccharide; Of Gram negative outer membrane"],["","Which bacteria have endotoxin?","Which bacteria have endotoxin?","Only Gram negatives"],["","Gram negative envelope: 3 outer parts?","Gram negative envelope: 3 outer parts?","Outer membrane; Lipopolysaccharide; Proteins and pores"]]},{"id":34069,"t":"Bacterial Envelope and Endotoxin","s":"A patient with Gram-negative bacteraemia develops a severe systemic inflammatory response. Which bacterial molecule is most directly responsible?","o":["Lipopolysaccharide, specifically lipid A","Teichoic acid of the cell wall","Peptidoglycan transpeptidase","The 70S ribosome","Dipicolinic acid of spores"],"e":"Endotoxin is the toxic lipopolysaccharide (LPS) of the Gram-negative outer membrane, specifically its lipid A component, which can trigger a strong inflammatory response in the host. Teichoic acid is a Gram-positive feature; transpeptidases build the wall; dipicolinic acid is in spores.","w":["Teichoic acid is Gram-positive.","Transpeptidase builds peptidoglycan.","Ribosomes make protein.","Dipicolinic acid maintains spore status."],"k":"Endotoxin = LPS, lipid A drives inflammation.","rn":[["","Which part of Lipopolysaccharide is the toxic part?","Which part of Lipopolysaccharide is the toxic part?","Lipid A"],["","What are the 2 parts of Lipopolysaccharide?","What are the 2 parts of Lipopolysaccharide?","O antigen; Lipid A"],["","What is lipid A?","What is lipid A?","Anchor for Lipopolysaccharide; Immunogenic"]]},{"id":34070,"t":"Bacterial Envelope and Endotoxin","s":"Lipopolysaccharide has two parts shown on the endotoxin slide. Which description is correct?","o":["O antigen: variable repeating glycan; lipid A: the anchor","O antigen: the membrane anchor; lipid A: the strain-variable sugar chain","O antigen: a ribosomal protein; lipid A: the capsule","O antigen: a peptide cross-link; lipid A: a porin","O antigen and lipid A are both secreted exotoxins"],"e":"LPS consists of the O antigen, a repeating glycan polymer that varies by strain and is immunogenic, drifting out from the bacterial surface, and lipid A, the anchor for LPS in the outer membrane, also immunogenic. Lipid A is the toxic component of endotoxin.","w":["The roles are reversed.","Neither is ribosomal or a capsule.","Neither is a peptide cross-link or porin.","LPS is endotoxin, part of the wall."],"k":"LPS = O antigen (variable glycan) + lipid A (anchor).","rn":[["","What is the O antigen of Lipopolysaccharide?","What is the O antigen of Lipopolysaccharide?","Repeating glycan polymer; Varies by strain; immunogenic"]]},{"id":34071,"t":"Bacterial Envelope and Endotoxin","s":"The strain-variable, immunogenic repeating sugar chain of LPS is called the ___ antigen. (Give the letter.)","a":["o","o antigen","o-antigen"],"e":"The O antigen is the repeating glycan polymer of LPS that varies by strain and is immunogenic; for example, <i>E. coli</i> O157 is named after its O antigen. Lipid A anchors LPS in the outer membrane.","k":"Strain-variable LPS sugar chain = O antigen.","rn":[["","What is the O antigen of Lipopolysaccharide?","What is the O antigen of Lipopolysaccharide?","Repeating glycan polymer; Varies by strain; immunogenic"]]},{"id":34072,"t":"Bacterial Envelope and Endotoxin","s":"Which toxin is given as the example of an exotoxin?","o":["Botulinum toxin","Lipid A","O antigen","Lipoteichoic acid","Mycolic acid"],"e":"Exotoxins are made and exported by bacteria (“exo” = comes out of), e.g. botulinum toxin; both Gram positives and Gram negatives make them. Lipid A and the O antigen are parts of endotoxin (LPS); lipoteichoic acid and mycolic acid are wall components.","w":["Lipid A is part of endotoxin.","The O antigen is part of LPS.","Lipoteichoic acid is a Gram-positive wall component.","Mycolic acid is the mycobacterial wall lipid."],"k":"Exotoxin example: botulinum toxin.","rn":[["","What is an example of an exotoxin?","What is an example of an exotoxin?","Botulism toxin"],["","What is an exotoxin?","What is an exotoxin?","Made and exported; By the bacterium; e.g. botulism toxin"]]},{"id":34073,"t":"Other Bacterial Features","s":"A child with an unencapsulated mutant strain of <i>Haemophilus influenzae</i> clears the infection easily, while the wild-type capsulated strain causes invasive disease. Why?","o":["It resists phagocytosis and complement lysis","The capsule contains lipid A that causes shock","The capsule makes the bacterium Gram positive","The capsule carries the 70S ribosomes","The capsule allows spore formation"],"e":"The capsule is a polysaccharide coat that “hides” the immunogenic cell wall and confers virulence, e.g. in <i>H. influenzae</i>, where it enables resistance to phagocytosis and complement-mediated lysis. Immunity requires antibodies to the capsule. It is a metabolic burden to the bacterium.","w":["Lipid A is in LPS, not the capsule.","Gram status is set by the wall.","Ribosomes are cytoplasmic.","Spores are made by <i>Bacillus</i>/<i>Clostridium</i>."],"k":"Capsule resists phagocytosis and complement.","rn":[["","What 2 things does the H. influenzae capsule resist?","What 2 things does the H. influenzae capsule resist?","Phagocytosis; Complement-mediated lysis"]]},{"id":34074,"t":"Other Bacterial Features","s":"Which statement about the bacterial capsule is NOT correct?","o":["It is metabolically cheap for the bacterium to make","It is a polysaccharide coat","It hides the immunogenic cell wall","Immunity requires antibodies against it","It confers virulence on <i>H. influenzae</i>"],"e":"The capsule is a polysaccharide coat that hides the immunogenic cell wall; immunity requires antibodies to the capsule, and it confers virulence (e.g. <i>H. influenzae</i>). It is a metabolic burden on the bacterium, not cheap.","w":["This is correct: it is polysaccharide.","This is correct.","This is correct.","This is correct."],"k":"Capsule is a metabolic burden."},{"id":34075,"t":"Other Bacterial Features","s":"Why must protective immunity against a capsulated organism target the capsule?","o":["It covers the wall, hiding wall antigens","The capsule is the only part containing DNA","The capsule is shed into the blood as an exotoxin","The capsule is identical in all bacteria","Capsules are made of peptidoglycan"],"e":"The capsule “hides” the immunogenic cell wall, so antibodies against wall components cannot reach their targets; immunity therefore requires antibodies to the capsule itself. Capsules are polysaccharide coats and differ between organisms.","w":["DNA is in the nucleoid and plasmids.","Capsules are not exotoxins.","Capsules vary between bacteria.","Capsules are polysaccharide."],"k":"Capsule hides wall → antibodies must target capsule."},{"id":34076,"t":"Other Bacterial Features","s":"Bacterial ribosomes are a selective antibiotic target because they:","o":["are 70S, smaller than eukaryotic 80S ribosomes","are 80S, larger than human 70S ribosomes","contain no RNA, only protein","are located inside mitochondria","are made of peptidoglycan"],"e":"Ribosomes are the engines of protein synthesis. Bacteria have 70S ribosomes (S = svedberg, sedimentation rate) with 50S and 30S subunits, each containing RNA and proteins, smaller than eukaryotic 80S. Bacterial RNA is a target of antibiotics and diagnostic tests.","w":["The sizes are reversed.","Each subunit contains RNA and proteins.","Bacteria have no mitochondria.","Ribosomes are RNA–protein machines."],"k":"Bacterial ribosome 70S = 50S + 30S.","rn":[["","Bacterial vs eukaryotic ribosome size?","Bacterial vs eukaryotic ribosome size?","Bacterial 70S; Eukaryotic 80S"]]},{"id":34077,"t":"Other Bacterial Features","s":"What does the “S” in 70S stand for?","o":["Svedberg unit, a measure of sedimentation rate","Subunit number","Sugar content","Size in nanometres","Synthesis rate of protein"],"e":"S is the svedberg unit (sedimentation rate), named after Theodor Svedberg (Nobel Prize 1926). Sedimentation values are not additive, which is why 50S + 30S subunits make a 70S ribosome.","w":["It is not a subunit count.","It is not sugar content.","It is not a size in nm.","It is not a synthesis rate."],"k":"S = svedberg (sedimentation).","rn":[["","What does S in 70S mean?","What does S in 70S mean?","Svedberg unit (sedimentation rate)"]]},{"id":34078,"t":"Other Bacterial Features","s":"Name the larger subunit of the bacterial 70S ribosome.","a":["50s","50 s","50s subunit","50"],"e":"The bacterial 70S ribosome has a 50S large subunit and a 30S small subunit, each containing RNA and protein. During translation, tRNAs carrying amino acids occupy the A, P and E sites as the mRNA (5′→3′) runs between the subunits.","k":"70S = 50S + 30S.","rn":[["","What are the 2 subunits of the bacterial ribosome?","What are the 2 subunits of the bacterial ribosome?","50S; 30S"]]},{"id":34079,"t":"Other Bacterial Features","s":"Besides being an antibiotic target, how is bacterial RNA used clinically?","o":["In diagnostic tests","As a vaccine adjuvant","As a source of exotoxin","To make the capsule","To stain mycobacteria"],"e":"Bacterial ribosomal RNA is a target of antibiotics and is also used in diagnostic tests. Molecular methods can identify specific strains beyond what staining and morphology show.","w":["Not described in the lecture.","RNA is not an exotoxin.","The capsule is polysaccharide.","Mycobacteria are identified with acid-fast stain."],"k":"Bacterial RNA: antibiotic target and diagnostic tool.","rn":[["","What are the 2 uses of bacterial RNA in medicine?","What are the 2 uses of bacterial RNA in medicine?","Target of antibiotics; Diagnostic tests"]]},{"id":34080,"t":"Other Bacterial Features","s":"An MRSA isolate transfers a resistance gene to a nearby susceptible <i>Staphylococcus</i> on circular, independently replicating DNA. What is this DNA, and what is the transfer process?","o":["A plasmid passed by conjugation","A transposon passed vertically by binary fission","The nucleoid passed by budding","A ribosome passed by phagocytosis","A spore passed by germination"],"e":"Plasmids are circular, extra-chromosomal, independently replicating DNA present in many bacteria, coding for dozens of genes including toxins and antibiotic resistance. Like viruses they are passed down to progeny (vertical), and some are transmitted between bacteria by conjugation (horizontal gene transfer).","w":["Binary fission is vertical; transposons move within genomes.","The nucleoid is the chromosome region and budding is yeast reproduction.","Ribosomes do not carry genes between cells.","Spores do not transfer genes."],"k":"Plasmid + conjugation = horizontal gene transfer.","rn":[["","What are the 2 ways plasmids are passed on?","What are the 2 ways plasmids are passed on?","To daughter cells (vertical); Between bacteria (horizontal, conjugation)"]]},{"id":34081,"t":"Other Bacterial Features","s":"Name the process by which plasmids are transferred directly between bacteria (horizontal gene transfer).","a":["conjugation","bacterial conjugation"],"e":"Plasmids are passed down to progeny (vertical transmission) and some are transmitted between bacteria (horizontal gene transfer) by conjugation. Plasmids and transposons both code for toxins and antibiotic-resistance genes, so this spreads resistance.","k":"Horizontal plasmid transfer = conjugation.","rn":[["","What are the 2 ways plasmids are passed on?","What are the 2 ways plasmids are passed on?","To daughter cells (vertical); Between bacteria (horizontal, conjugation)"]]},{"id":34082,"t":"Other Bacterial Features","s":"Which feature distinguishes a transposon from a plasmid?","o":["It can move location in the genome and encodes transposase","It is circular and replicates independently","It is only passed vertically to daughter cells","It cannot carry antibiotic resistance genes","It is found only in eukaryotic cells"],"e":"Transposons are DNA sequences able to move location in the genome; they encode transposase plus other genes and move between genomic and plasmid DNA, between plasmids, and from plasmid to genomic DNA. Plasmids are circular and independently replicating. Both code for toxins and antibiotic-resistance genes.","w":["That describes plasmids.","Transposons move between DNA molecules, not just vertically.","Both carry resistance genes.","They occur in bacteria."],"k":"Transposon: mobile DNA encoding transposase.","rn":[["","What is a transposon?","What is a transposon?","DNA that moves location; Encodes transposase"]]},{"id":34083,"t":"Other Bacterial Features","s":"Why can DNA move between the chromosome and mobile elements more readily in bacteria than in human cells?","o":["It has no nucleus, so DNA processes run alongside the rest of the cell","Bacteria have more mitochondria to supply energy","Bacterial DNA is single-stranded","Bacteria have 80S ribosomes that copy DNA","Bacterial DNA is protected by a capsule"],"e":"Because bacterial DNA is not enclosed in a membrane-bound nucleus, replication, transcription and translation happen next to other cellular processes, and DNA can be more readily transferred between chromosomal DNA and mobile elements such as plasmids and transposons.","w":["Bacteria lack mitochondria.","Bacterial chromosomal DNA is not described as single-stranded.","Bacteria have 70S ribosomes, which make protein.","The capsule is an outer polysaccharide coat."],"k":"No nucleus → easy exchange with mobile elements.","rn":[["","Why can bacterial DNA move easily to mobile elements?","Why can bacterial DNA move easily to mobile elements?","No membrane-bound nucleus"]]},{"id":34084,"t":"Other Bacterial Features","s":"Which two products are encoded by both plasmids and transposons and matter most clinically?","o":["Toxins and antibiotic resistance genes","Capsules and spores","Ribosomes and mitochondria","Peptidoglycan and LPS","Porins and flagella"],"e":"Both plasmids and transposons code for toxins and antibiotic resistance genes. Mobile genetic elements can therefore spread traits, including antimicrobial resistance, between bacteria, which is why they matter in nosocomial organisms such as MRSA and VRE.","w":["Not stated as their products.","Bacteria lack mitochondria.","Wall components are chromosomally encoded structures.","Not stated as their clinically key products."],"k":"Mobile elements carry toxin and resistance genes.","rn":[["","What 2 things do mobile genetic elements code for?","What 2 things do mobile genetic elements code for?","Toxins; Antibiotic resistance genes"]]},{"id":34085,"t":"Other Bacterial Features","s":"An outbreak of diarrhoea on a ward persists despite alcohol hand gel and routine disinfection. A spore-forming organism is suspected. Why does this matter for infection control?","o":["Spores resist disinfection, so sterilisation by prolonged heat is needed","Spores are killed by alcohol gel within seconds","Spores divide rapidly and must be treated with antibiotics only","Spores are only formed by Gram-negative bacteria","Spores are metabolically active and need oxygen"],"e":"Some medically important bacteria (<i>Bacillus</i> spp. and <i>Clostridium</i> spp.) make spores: non-replicating, dormant, metabolically inactive forms resistant to drying, temperature, disinfection and digestion. They matter for clinical disease patterns and infection control, where prolonged high temperatures (sterilisation, not disinfection) are needed.","w":["Spores resist disinfection.","Spores are dormant and non-replicating.","<i>Bacillus</i> and <i>Clostridium</i> are Gram-positive rods.","Spores are metabolically inactive."],"k":"Spores resist disinfection; need sterilisation.","rn":[["","How are spores killed in infection control?","How are spores killed in infection control?","Sterilisation (prolonged high temperature); Not disinfection"]]},{"id":34086,"t":"Other Bacterial Features","s":"Which pair of genera make spores?","o":["<i>Bacillus</i> and <i>Clostridium</i>","<i>Staphylococcus</i> and <i>Streptococcus</i>","<i>Neisseria</i> and <i>Moraxella</i>","<i>Mycoplasma</i> and <i>Chlamydia</i>","<i>Escherichia</i> and <i>Klebsiella</i>"],"e":"<i>Bacillus</i> spp. and <i>Clostridium</i> spp. are the spore-formers named; the slide shows endospores in <i>Clostridium botulinum</i> with a spore stain. Spores reactivate when conditions become favourable.","w":["These cocci are not spore-formers.","These are Gram-negative cocci.","These lack a cell wall.","These Gram-negative rods are not spore-formers."],"k":"Spore-formers: Bacillus, Clostridium.","rn":[["","Which 2 genera of medically important bacteria make spores?","Which 2 genera of medically important bacteria make spores?","Bacillus; Clostridium"]]},{"id":34087,"t":"Other Bacterial Features","s":"What do bacterial spores contain?","o":["DNA, ribosomes and dipicolinic acid","Mitochondria, a nucleus and lipid A","Only peptidoglycan with no genetic material","Lipopolysaccharide and porins only","Actively dividing daughter cells"],"e":"Spores contain the DNA, ribosomes and dipicolinic acid (which helps maintain spore status). They resist very high and low temperatures, can last thousands of years (some reports of millions), and reactivate when conditions are favourable.","w":["Bacteria lack mitochondria and a nucleus.","Spores contain DNA.","LPS and porins are outer-membrane parts.","Spores are non-replicating."],"k":"Spore = DNA + ribosomes + dipicolinic acid.","rn":[["","What do spores contain?","What do spores contain?","DNA; Ribosomes; Dipicolinic acid"]]},{"id":34088,"t":"Other Bacterial Features","s":"Name the molecule inside bacterial spores that helps maintain the spore state.","a":["dipicolinic acid","dpa","dipicolinic"],"e":"Spores contain DNA, ribosomes and dipicolinic acid, which helps maintain spore status. Spores are dormant, metabolically inactive and highly resistant; they reactivate (germinate) when conditions are right.","k":"Dipicolinic acid maintains spores.","rn":[["","What do spores contain?","What do spores contain?","DNA; Ribosomes; Dipicolinic acid"],["","What does dipicolinic acid do?","What does dipicolinic acid do?","Keeps spore status"]]},{"id":34089,"t":"Other Bacterial Features","s":"How does bacterial gene regulation contribute to disease?","o":["They sense conditions and switch on adhesins and damaging enzymes","Bacteria permanently express every gene at maximum level","Gene regulation only affects spore colour","Bacteria use gene regulation to make human antibodies","Gene regulation prevents bacteria responding to nutrients"],"e":"Bacteria sense and adjust to their environment, altering growth rate and metabolic pathways, and regulate virulence factors: adhesion molecules and enzymes that degrade host proteins, degrade immune mediators or lyse host cells. This lets them rapidly change behaviour in response to conditions.","w":["Genes are switched on and off.","It controls far more than spores.","Antibodies are made by the host.","Gene regulation responds to nutrients."],"k":"Gene regulation controls virulence factors."},{"id":34090,"t":"Other Bacterial Features","s":"Which is NOT listed as a type of virulence factor that bacteria regulate?","o":["Enzymes that synthesise host antibodies","Adhesion molecules","Enzymes that degrade host proteins","Enzymes that degrade immune mediators","Enzymes that lyse host cells"],"e":"Regulated virulence factors include adhesion molecules and enzymes to degrade host proteins, degrade immune mediators and lyse host cells. Bacteria do not make host antibodies.","w":["Adhesion molecules are listed.","Protein-degrading enzymes are listed.","Immune-mediator-degrading enzymes are listed.","Lytic enzymes are listed."],"k":"Virulence factors: adhesins, degradative and lytic enzymes.","rn":[["","What are the 3 jobs of bacterial virulence enzymes?","What are the 3 jobs of bacterial virulence enzymes?","Degrade host proteins; Degrade immune mediators; Lyse host cells"]]},{"id":34091,"t":"Bacterial Envelope and Endotoxin","s":"Which structure would NOT be labelled on a drawing of a typical prokaryotic cell?","o":["Nuclear envelope","Plasmid","Nucleoid","Flagellum or pilus","Capsule"],"e":"A typical prokaryotic cell contains a capsule (in some), cell wall, plasma membrane, nucleoid, plasmids, ribosomes, cytoplasm and flagella or pili. It lacks a nuclear envelope; its DNA lies in the nucleoid.","w":["Plasmids are typical.","The nucleoid holds the DNA.","Flagella or pili are typical.","Capsules occur in many bacteria."],"k":"Prokaryotes have no nuclear envelope.","rn":[["","Why does bacterial DNA transfer easily?","Why does bacterial DNA transfer easily?","DNA next to other processes; No nuclear membrane"]]},{"id":34092,"t":"Bacterial Envelope and Endotoxin","s":"A Gram stain of a blood culture shows pink rods. Which combination of features would you expect this organism to have?","o":["Thin peptidoglycan, outer membrane and endotoxin","Thick peptidoglycan, teichoic acid and no endotoxin","Mycolic acid wall and acid-fast staining","No cell wall and no Gram reaction","A nucleus and 80S ribosomes"],"e":"Pink = Gram negative: thin peptidoglycan plus an outer membrane with LPS (endotoxin), porins and proteins. Purple Gram positives have thick peptidoglycan with teichoic/lipoteichoic acid and no endotoxin. Mycobacteria do not Gram stain; <i>Mycoplasma</i> lack a wall.","w":["That describes Gram positives (purple).","That describes mycobacteria.","That describes <i>Mycoplasma</i>.","Bacteria have no nucleus and have 70S ribosomes."],"k":"Pink rods = Gram-negative: thin wall, outer membrane, LPS.","rn":[["","Endotoxin","Endotoxin","Lipopolysaccharide; Of Gram negative outer membrane"],["","Gram negative envelope: 2 inner parts?","Gram negative envelope: 2 inner parts?","Thin peptidoglycan; Inner membrane"]]},{"id":34093,"t":"Bacterial Envelope and Endotoxin","s":"A Gram stain shows purple cocci in clusters from a post-operative wound. What is the most likely organism and the expected wall structure?","o":["<i>S. aureus</i>; thick peptidoglycan, no outer membrane","<i>S. pyogenes</i>; thick peptidoglycan, outer membrane","<i>N. meningitidis</i>; thin peptidoglycan, outer membrane","<i>E. coli</i>; thin peptidoglycan, LPS","<i>M. tuberculosis</i>; mycolic acid layer"],"e":"Purple = Gram positive (thick peptidoglycan retains crystal violet; no outer membrane). Clusters = staphylococci; <i>S. aureus</i> (including MRSA) is a key cause of nosocomial wound infection. Streptococci form chains; <i>N. meningitidis</i> and <i>E. coli</i> are Gram negative.","w":["<i>S. pyogenes</i> forms chains and Gram positives lack an outer membrane.","<i>N. meningitidis</i> is a pink coccus.","<i>E. coli</i> is a pink rod.","Mycobacteria do not Gram stain."],"k":"Purple cocci in clusters = staphylococci (S. aureus).","rn":[["","Endotoxin","Endotoxin","Lipopolysaccharide; Of Gram negative outer membrane"],["","Which Gram type has an outer membrane?","Which Gram type has an outer membrane?","Gram negative"],["","Gram negative envelope: 3 outer parts?","Gram negative envelope: 3 outer parts?","Outer membrane; Lipopolysaccharide; Proteins and pores"]]},{"id":34094,"t":"Bacterial Classification","s":"Which pair of organisms shares the same Gram reaction AND shape?","o":["<i>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</i> and <i>Bordetella pertussis</i>","<i>Staphylococcus aureus</i> and <i>Neisseria meningitidis</i>","<i>Bacillus anthracis</i> and <i>Escherichia coli</i>","<i>Streptococcus pneumoniae</i> and <i>Haemophilus influenzae</i>","<i>Moraxella catarrhalis</i> and <i>Corynebacterium</i>"],"e":"<i>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</i> and <i>Bordetella pertussis</i> are both Gram-negative rods. <i>S. aureus</i> (Gram-positive coccus) vs <i>N. meningitidis</i> (Gram-negative coccus); <i>B. anthracis</i> (Gram-positive rod) vs <i>E. coli</i> (Gram-negative rod); <i>S. pneumoniae</i> (Gram-positive coccus) vs <i>H. influenzae</i> (Gram-negative rod); <i>Moraxella</i> (Gram-negative coccus) vs <i>Corynebacterium</i> (Gram-positive rod).","w":["Different Gram reactions.","Different Gram reactions.","Different Gram reaction and shape.","Different Gram reaction and shape."],"k":"Pseudomonas and Bordetella: both Gram-negative rods.","rn":[["","Name the Gram negative rods (other than Enterobacteriaciae).","Name the Gram negative rods (other than Enterobacteriaciae).","Haemophilus influenzae; Pseudomonas aeruginosa; Bacteroides fragilis (strict anaerobe)"],["","More Gram negative rods?","More Gram negative rods?","Campylobacter and Helicobacter; Vibrio cholerae; Bordetella pertussis; Legionella pneumophila"]]},{"id":34095,"t":"Other Bacterial Features","s":"Which organism is correctly matched with a key feature from the lecture?","o":["<i>Clostridium botulinum</i>: endospores","<i>Haemophilus influenzae</i>: mycolic acid wall","<i>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</i>: no cell wall","<i>Mycoplasma pneumoniae</i>: thick peptidoglycan","<i>Treponema pallidum</i>: grows on glucose and salts only"],"e":"<i>C. botulinum</i> is shown forming endospores on a spore stain. <i>H. influenzae</i>'s key feature is its virulence-conferring capsule; <i>M. tuberculosis</i> has a mycolic-acid wall; <i>Mycoplasma</i> lacks a wall; <i>E. coli</i>, not <i>T. pallidum</i>, grows on glucose and salts.","w":["<i>H. influenzae</i>'s feature is its capsule.","<i>M. tuberculosis</i> has a wall with mycolic acid.","<i>Mycoplasma</i> has no wall.","<i>T. pallidum</i> needs enriched medium."],"k":"C. botulinum forms endospores.","rn":[["","Image card: What are the structures inside these rods? Which genus makes them?","What are the structures inside these rods? Which genus makes them?","Endospores; Clostridium (C. botulinum)"]]},{"id":34096,"t":"Other Bacterial Features","s":"A new antibiotic binds the 30S subunit. Which statement predicts its effect?","o":["It blocks bacterial translation but spares 80S ribosomes","It blocks peptidoglycan cross-linking","It neutralises lipid A in the bloodstream","It dissolves the mycolic acid layer","It prevents conjugation of plasmids only"],"e":"The 30S is the small subunit of the bacterial 70S ribosome (50S + 30S), the engine of protein synthesis. Because human ribosomes are 80S, bacterial ribosomal components are a selective antibiotic target. Peptidoglycan cross-linking is done by transpeptidases.","w":["That is a transpeptidase target.","Ribosomal drugs do not neutralise LPS.","Mycolic acid is not ribosomal.","The ribosome is not the conjugation machinery."],"k":"30S/50S targets block bacterial translation selectively.","rn":[["","Bacterial vs eukaryotic ribosome size?","Bacterial vs eukaryotic ribosome size?","Bacterial 70S; Eukaryotic 80S"],["","What are the 5 other important bacterial features?","What are the 5 other important bacterial features?","Capsule; ribosomes; Mobile genetic elements; Spores; gene regulation"]]},{"id":34097,"t":"Other Bacterial Features","s":"Which pair of bacterial features both help bacteria survive harsh conditions or evade host defences?","o":["Capsules and endospores","70S ribosomes and binary fission","Plasma membrane and cytoplasm","Glucose uptake and inorganic salts","Lag phase and nucleoid"],"e":"Capsules help bacteria evade host defences (resisting phagocytosis and complement), and endospores let certain bacteria survive drying, heat, disinfection and digestion. Mobile genetic elements spread traits including resistance, and gene regulation lets bacteria rapidly alter behaviour.","w":["These are basic protein synthesis and reproduction features.","These are universal cell components.","These are nutritional needs.","These are a growth phase and DNA region."],"k":"Capsules evade defence; spores survive harsh conditions.","rn":[["","Image card: What are the structures inside these rods? Which genus makes them?","What are the structures inside these rods? Which genus makes them?","Endospores; Clostridium (C. botulinum)"]]},{"id":34098,"t":"Other Bacterial Features","s":"Which combination is used to identify bacteria clinically?","o":["Microscopy, culture, biochemistry and molecular tests","Antibody titres alone","Patient age and symptoms alone","Only whole-genome sequencing","Only the shape of colonies on a plate"],"e":"Identification matters for diagnosis and choice of treatment. Bacteria are identified using a combination of microscopy and staining, culture characteristics, biochemical tests and molecular methods; staining and morphology are the main initial investigations, and advanced biochemical and molecular methods can identify strains.","w":["Antibody titres are not the main tool.","Clinical features alone cannot identify bacteria.","Sequencing is not the only method.","Colony shape alone is not enough."],"k":"ID: microscopy/stain, culture, biochemistry, molecular.","rn":[["","Identifying bacteria: 2 other methods?","Identifying bacteria: 2 other methods?","Biochemical tests; Molecular methods"],["","Identifying bacteria: 2 lab methods?","Identifying bacteria: 2 lab methods?","Microscopy and staining; Culture characteristics"],["","What can biochemical tests and molecular methods identify?","What can biochemical tests and molecular methods identify?","Specific strains of infectious agents"]]},{"id":34099,"t":"Bacterial Classification","s":"Why is the Gram stain an important initial laboratory investigation?","o":["It sorts bacteria by envelope and shows shape and arrangement","It identifies the exact strain, e.g. O157:H7","It measures antibiotic resistance directly","It shows whether the organism carries plasmids","It detects endospores in all bacteria"],"e":"The Gram stain is a differential stain that separates bacteria primarily by their cell envelope (thick peptidoglycan, purple vs thin peptidoglycan + outer membrane, pink) and also reveals morphology and arrangement (e.g. clusters vs chains), making it an important first test. Strain identification needs biochemical or molecular methods.","w":["Strains need advanced methods.","Resistance is not measured by Gram stain.","Plasmids are not seen on Gram stain.","A separate spore stain is used."],"k":"Gram stain: envelope type + shape + arrangement.","rn":[["","What does a Gram stain also reveal besides staining colour?","What does a Gram stain also reveal besides staining colour?","Bacterial shape and arrangement; Important first lab investigation"]]},{"id":34100,"t":"Infection and Human Disease","s":"Which definition of infection is used in the lecture summary?","o":["Invasion of host tissues, with or without disease","Any presence of bacteria on the skin","Disease caused only by bacteria","A condition in which the immune system attacks self","Colonisation of the gut by normal flora"],"e":"Infection is the invasion of host tissues by foreign organisms which may or may not cause disease. It can be caused by bacteria (e.g. TB, plague), viruses (common cold, influenza), fungi (thrush, athlete's foot), protozoa (sleeping sickness, malaria) and multicellular parasites (tapeworm, flea).","w":["Skin flora are not necessarily infection.","Viruses, fungi and parasites also cause infection.","That describes autoimmunity.","Normal gut flora are commensal."],"k":"Infection = invasion of host tissue, may or may not cause disease.","rn":[["","What is infection?","What is infection?","Invasion of host tissues; By foreign organisms; Maybe disease, maybe not"]]},{"id":34101,"t":"Infection and Human Disease","s":"Which pairing of microbial group with an example infection is correct?","o":["Protozoa: sleeping sickness","Fungi: tuberculosis","Viruses: athlete's foot","Bacteria: influenza","Multicellular parasites: plague"],"e":"Summary examples: bacteria (tuberculosis, plague); viruses (common cold, influenza); fungi (thrush, athlete's foot); protozoa (sleeping sickness, malaria); multicellular parasites (tapeworm, flea).","w":["TB is bacterial.","Athlete's foot is fungal.","Influenza is viral.","Plague is bacterial."],"k":"Protozoa cause sleeping sickness and malaria.","rn":[["","What are examples of protozoal infections?","What are examples of protozoal infections?","Sleeping sickness; Malaria"]]},{"id":34102,"t":"Infection and Human Disease","s":"A 30-year-old with no prior disease develops <i>C. difficile</i> diarrhoea after antibiotics. Which concept from the lecture best explains why?","o":["Antibiotics disturb the protective, competing microbiome","Antibiotics directly convert normal flora into toxins","Antibiotics create spores inside human cells","Antibiotics increase the 80S ribosome count","Antibiotics remove the capsule from all bacteria"],"e":"Normal flora compete with harmful organisms for nutrients and the environment, protecting us. Disturbing the microbiome can lead to disease, and <i>C. difficile</i> is listed among nosocomial infections. <i>Clostridium</i> species form spores that resist disinfection, aiding spread on wards.","w":["Flora are not converted into toxins.","Spores are bacterial structures.","Antibiotics do not change human ribosomes.","This is not the mechanism."],"k":"Microbiome disturbance permits pathogens.","rn":[["","What can disturbing the microbiome lead to?","What can disturbing the microbiome lead to?","Disease"],["","How does the microbiome protect us?","How does the microbiome protect us?","Competes with harmful microbes; For nutrients and environment"]]},{"id":34103,"t":"Koch's Postulates","s":"An investigator applies Koch's postulates today. What replaces “animal” in the modern application?","o":["Patients","Cell lines only","Culture plates","Computer models","Insects"],"e":"Koch worked on animals; today “animal” is replaced by patients, but pretty much the same criteria are still followed to establish the cause of an infectious disease: association, isolation in pure culture, reproduction of disease and re-isolation.","w":["Not the stated replacement.","Culture plates are used for postulate 2, not as hosts.","Not stated.","Insects are vectors, not hosts in the postulates."],"k":"Modern Koch's postulates reason from patients.","rn":[["","Why do Koch's postulates still apply?","Why do Koch's postulates still apply?","Same criteria establish cause; Patients replace animals"],["","Koch's postulates 3 and 4?","Koch's postulates 3 and 4?","Causes disease in healthy animal; Re-isolated from new host"]]},{"id":34104,"t":"Infection and Human Disease","s":"Which difference between prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells provides antimicrobial drug targets?","o":["No nucleus or organelles; 70S ribosomes; peptidoglycan","Prokaryotes have larger ribosomes than eukaryotes","Eukaryotes lack a plasma membrane","Prokaryotes contain mitochondria that eukaryotes lack","Eukaryotes have cell walls made of peptidoglycan"],"e":"Prokaryotic cells lack a membrane-bound nucleus and organelles (DNA in the nucleoid); eukaryotic cells have a nucleus and organelles such as mitochondria. Bacteria have 70S ribosomes and peptidoglycan; these differences provide important targets for antimicrobial drugs.","w":["Bacterial ribosomes are smaller.","Eukaryotes have a plasma membrane.","Prokaryotes lack mitochondria.","Peptidoglycan is bacterial."],"k":"Prokaryote–eukaryote differences = drug targets.","rn":[["","Prokaryote vs eukaryote (drug targets)","Prokaryote vs eukaryote (drug targets)","Prokaryote: no nucleus/organelles; Eukaryote: nucleus, mitochondria"]]},{"id":34105,"t":"Bacterial Classification","s":"A patient with peptic ulcer disease has a Gram-negative rod identified on biopsy. Which genus on the medically important list fits?","o":["<i>Helicobacter</i>","<i>Peptostreptococcus</i>","<i>Corynebacterium</i>","<i>Moraxella</i>","<i>Enterococcus</i>"],"e":"<i>Campylobacter</i> and <i>Helicobacter</i> are listed together among the Gram-negative rods. <i>Peptostreptococcus</i> are strict-anaerobic Gram-positive cocci, <i>Corynebacterium</i> a Gram-positive rod, <i>Moraxella</i> a Gram-negative coccus and enterococci Gram-positive cocci.","w":["Gram-positive anaerobic cocci.","Gram-positive rod.","Gram-negative coccus.","Gram-positive coccus."],"k":"Helicobacter = Gram-negative rod.","rn":[["","More Gram negative rods?","More Gram negative rods?","Campylobacter and Helicobacter; Vibrio cholerae; Bordetella pertussis; Legionella pneumophila"]]},{"id":34106,"t":"Bacterial Classification","s":"Which group do <i>Salmonella</i>, <i>Shigella</i>, <i>E. coli</i>, <i>Klebsiella</i>, <i>Enterobacter</i> and <i>Proteus</i> belong to?","a":["enterobacteriaceae","enterobacteria","enterobacterales"],"e":"On the medically important list, the Enterobacteriaceae are Gram-negative rods: <i>Salmonella</i> and <i>Shigella</i>, <i>Escherichia coli</i>, <i>Klebsiella</i> and <i>Enterobacter</i>, and <i>Proteus</i>.","k":"Enterobacteriaceae = family of Gram-negative rods."},{"id":34107,"t":"Bacterial Classification","s":"Which statement about the streptococci on the medically important list is correct?","o":["Group A is <i>S. pyogenes</i> and Group D is enterococcus","Group A is enterococcus and Group D is <i>S. pyogenes</i>","All streptococci are strict anaerobes","Streptococci are Gram-negative cocci in clusters","<i>S. pneumoniae</i> is the meningococcus"],"e":"Streptococci on the list: Group A (<i>S. pyogenes</i>), Group B, Groups C and G, Group D (enterococcus), <i>S. pneumoniae</i> (pneumococcus) and viridans-type streptococci. They are Gram-positive cocci in chains; the strict anaerobic Gram-positive cocci are <i>Peptococcus/Peptostreptococcus</i>.","w":["The groups are swapped.","Only <i>Peptococcus/Peptostreptococcus</i> are listed as strict anaerobes.","Streptococci are Gram positive and form chains.","<i>S. pneumoniae</i> is pneumococcus."],"k":"Group A = S. pyogenes; Group D = enterococcus.","rn":[["","Name the Lancefield streptococcus groups","Name the Lancefield streptococcus groups","A (S. pyogenes); B, C and G; D (= enterococcus)"]]},{"id":34108,"t":"Bacterial Classification","s":"Which shapes are the two most important for identifying medically relevant bacteria?","o":["Cocci and bacilli","Vibrios and spirochetes","Spirilla and coccobacilli","Filamentous and branched forms","Comma-shaped and spiral"],"e":"Most medically important bacteria fit the first two categories, cocci (round) and bacilli (rods), the “important ones” to focus on. A few are spiral, branched (filamentous) or comma-shaped (vibrio). Combined with the Gram stain they give four main groups.","w":["These are less common shapes.","Less common.","Only a few are filamentous/branched.","Less common."],"k":"Cocci and bacilli are the key shapes.","rn":[["","Image card: What is the Gram stain and shape of these bacteria?","What is the Gram stain and shape of these bacteria?","Gram negative; Bacilli"],["","Image card: What is the Gram stain and shape of these bacteria?","What is the Gram stain and shape of these bacteria?","Gram positive; Bacilli"],["","Image card: What is the Gram stain and shape of these bacteria?","What is the Gram stain and shape of these bacteria?","Gram negative; Cocci"]]},{"id":34109,"t":"Bacterial Classification","s":"Combining shape and Gram stain gives how many main groups of bacteria?","o":["Four","Two","Three","Six","Eight"],"e":"Combining shape (cocci or bacilli) with Gram reaction (positive or negative) gives four groups: Gram-positive cocci, Gram-negative bacilli, Gram-positive bacilli and Gram-negative cocci. The medically important bacteria table is organised this way.","w":["Two would ignore one of the variables.","Three groups would miss one combination.","Six counts other shapes.","Eight is not the classification used."],"k":"2 shapes × 2 Gram reactions = 4 groups.","rn":[["","What are the Gram positive cocci groups?","What are the Gram positive cocci groups?","Staphylococci; Streptococci; Peptococcus / Peptostreptococcus"]]},{"id":34110,"t":"Other Bacterial Features","s":"A drawing of a bacterium labels a layer outside the cell wall made of polysaccharide. What is it called?","a":["capsule","the capsule","polysaccharide capsule"],"e":"The capsule is the outer polysaccharide coat present in some bacteria. It hides the immunogenic cell wall, needs specific antibodies for immunity, is a metabolic burden and confers virulence, e.g. in <i>H. influenzae</i>.","k":"Outer polysaccharide coat = capsule.","rn":[["","What is the bacterial capsule made of?","What is the bacterial capsule made of?","Polysaccharide"],["","What does the capsule hide?","What does the capsule hide?","Immunogenic cell wall"]]},{"id":34111,"t":"Peptidoglycan and Mycobacteria","s":"Which step of peptidoglycan synthesis is matched with its purpose?","o":["Polymerisation of sugars → makes the backbone","Transpeptidase → adds sugars","Elongation of side-chains → cross-links strands","Polymerisation of sugars → adds peptides","Transpeptidase → makes the LPS anchor"],"e":"Three antibiotic-targetable steps: polymerisation of sugars to make the backbone, elongation of amino-acid side chains to add the peptides, and transpeptidase to cross-link. All steps are unique to bacteria.","w":["Transpeptidase cross-links.","Elongation adds peptides.","Polymerisation makes the backbone.","Lipid A anchors LPS and is unrelated to transpeptidase."],"k":"Sugars → backbone; elongation → peptides; transpeptidase → cross-links.","rn":[["","Which peptidoglycan synthesis steps do antibiotics target?","Which peptidoglycan synthesis steps do antibiotics target?","All of them; Sugar polymerisation, peptides, cross-linking"],["","What are the 3 steps of peptidoglycan synthesis?","What are the 3 steps of peptidoglycan synthesis?","Sugars polymerise (backbone); Peptide side-chains elongate; Transpeptidase cross-links"]]},{"id":34112,"t":"Peptidoglycan and Mycobacteria","s":"Why are the amino acids in peptidoglycan side-chains important for the bacterium?","o":["They are unusual and resist enzymatic breakdown","They are identical to human amino acids and easily digested","They make up the lipid A anchor","They form the capsule polysaccharide","They store energy for spore formation"],"e":"Peptidoglycan contains 3–5 amino-acid peptides made of amino acids peculiar to peptidoglycan and resistant to enzymatic destruction, adding to the wall's role in protection and structural integrity.","w":["They are peculiar to peptidoglycan and resist enzymes.","Lipid A is a lipid.","The capsule is polysaccharide.","They are structural."],"k":"Peptidoglycan peptides resist enzymatic destruction."},{"id":34113,"t":"Infection and Human Disease","s":"Which of the following is NOT listed as a nosocomial infection?","o":["Malaria","Nosocomial pneumonia","Wound infection","Implant/device-related infection","<i>Clostridium difficile</i>"],"e":"Nosocomial (hospital-acquired) examples: <i>S. aureus</i> including MRSA, <i>Enterococcus</i> including VRE, <i>C. difficile</i>, nosocomial pneumonia, wound infections and implant/device-related infections. Malaria is a vector-borne protozoal disease.","w":["Listed.","Listed.","Listed.","Listed."],"k":"Malaria is not a nosocomial example.","rn":[["","What are examples of protozoal infections?","What are examples of protozoal infections?","Sleeping sickness; Malaria"]]},{"id":34114,"t":"Infection and Human Disease","s":"Which resistant organism is described by the abbreviation MRSA?","a":["methicillin-resistant staphylococcus aureus","methicillin resistant staphylococcus aureus","methicillin resistant s aureus","meticillin-resistant staphylococcus aureus","methicillin-resistant s. aureus"],"e":"MRSA is methicillin-resistant <i>Staphylococcus aureus</i>, a Gram-positive coccus in clusters and a key nosocomial pathogen; maps show its prevalence varies widely between countries. Resistance genes can spread on plasmids and transposons.","k":"MRSA = methicillin-resistant S. aureus.","rn":[["","What is MRSA (methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus)?","What is MRSA (methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus)?","Staphylococcus aureus resistant to oxacillin"]]},{"id":34115,"t":"Infection and Human Disease","s":"How did plague shape human evolution according to the lecture?","o":["Survivors with protective gene variants passed them on","Plague introduced new chromosomes into the human genome","Plague caused humans to lose their microbiome","Plague made all Europeans immune to tuberculosis","Plague replaced human ribosomes with bacterial ones"],"e":"During the Black Death some people had a slight mutation in a gene that let the immune system fight the infection; they survived and their descendants are among us, so some people today may carry that variant. Infections have been hugely important throughout human history and evolution.","w":["No new chromosomes were introduced.","The microbiome was not lost.","No link to TB immunity was made.","Human ribosomes were not replaced."],"k":"Plague selected protective gene variants.","rn":[["","How did the Black Death affect human evolution?","How did the Black Death affect human evolution?","Survivors had a gene mutation; Helped immunity, passed to descendants"]]},{"id":34116,"t":"Taxonomy","s":"In <i>S. pyogenes</i> M3T3, what do the M and T letters refer to?","o":["M protein and T protein","Mitochondria and transposons","Mycolic acid and teichoic acid","Membrane and toxin","Morphology and temperature"],"e":"Strain names add detail within a species. For <i>S. pyogenes</i> M3T3, M3 refers to the M protein and T3 to the T protein; for <i>E. coli</i> O157:H7, O157 is the O antigen and H7 is linked on the slide to haemolytic uraemic syndrome.","w":["Bacteria lack mitochondria.","Mycolic acid is mycobacterial.","Not the meanings given.","Not the meanings given."],"k":"M3T3 = M protein and T protein.","rn":[["","In S. pyogenes M3T3, what do M3 and T3 refer to?","In S. pyogenes M3T3, what do M3 and T3 refer to?","M3: M protein; T3: T protein"]]},{"id":34117,"t":"Taxonomy","s":"A child infected with <i>E. coli</i> O157:H7 develops reduced urine output. What does haemolytic uraemic syndrome involve?","o":["Renal small-vessel damage causing acute kidney failure","Destruction of the bacterial capsule in the kidney","Formation of spores in the bladder","Blockage of the ureters by bacterial colonies","A purely cosmetic rash with no organ damage"],"e":"Haemolytic uraemic syndrome is damage to small blood vessels in the kidney, which can cause acute failure and long-term damage. It is linked to the O157:H7 strain of <i>E. coli</i>, which is why strain identification is clinically important.","w":["HUS damages host vessels.","<i>E. coli</i> does not form spores.","It is vascular damage, not obstruction.","It causes serious kidney damage."],"k":"HUS: renal small-vessel damage, acute kidney failure.","rn":[["","What is haemolytic uraemic syndrome?","What is haemolytic uraemic syndrome?","Damage to kidney small vessels"],["","What can haemolytic uraemic syndrome cause?","What can haemolytic uraemic syndrome cause?","Acute kidney failure; Long-term damage"]]},{"id":34118,"t":"Bacterial Classification","s":"A specimen is decolourised for far too long with alcohol/acetone. Which error might follow?","o":["Gram-positive organisms lose crystal violet and appear falsely pink","Gram-negative organisms retain crystal violet and appear purple","All organisms become acid fast","Capsules dissolve, revealing spores","Safranin stains only the Gram-positive organisms"],"e":"Decolourisation removes crystal violet from cells whose thin peptidoglycan cannot retain it (Gram negatives). Uptake and retention depend on the thickness and accessibility of the wall peptidoglycan, so excessive decolourisation could strip dye even from thick-walled cells, which would then take up the pink safranin.","w":["Over-decolourisation removes, not adds, violet.","Acid-fastness relates to mycolic acid.","Not an effect of decolourisation.","Safranin stains all decolourised cells."],"k":"Retention of violet depends on thick peptidoglycan.","rn":[["","Gram staining: first 2 steps?","Gram staining: first 2 steps?","Dry on glass plate; Crystal violet, set with iodine"],["","Gram positive vs Gram negative: colour?","Gram positive vs Gram negative: colour?","Positive: purple; Negative: pink; MNEMONIC"],["","Gram positive vs Gram negative: peptidoglycan?","Gram positive vs Gram negative: peptidoglycan?","Positive: thick; Negative: thin"]]},{"id":34119,"t":"Other Bacterial Features","s":"Which method is required to destroy bacterial spores on surgical instruments?","o":["Sterilisation with prolonged high temperatures","Routine surface disinfection","Drying the instruments in air","A short rinse in cold water","Exposure to low pH briefly"],"e":"Spores resist drying, temperature, disinfection and digestion. For infection control, prolonged high temperatures are needed: sterilisation, not disinfection. This matters for spore-formers such as <i>Bacillus</i> and <i>Clostridium</i> species.","w":["Spores resist disinfection.","Spores resist drying.","Inadequate.","Spores are highly resistant."],"k":"Spores need sterilisation.","rn":[["","How are spores killed in infection control?","How are spores killed in infection control?","Sterilisation (prolonged high temperature); Not disinfection"]]},{"id":34120,"t":"Other Bacterial Features","s":"A Gram-negative rod carrying a plasmid with a resistance gene is exposed to an antibiotic. Which outcome best reflects lecture concepts?","o":["Resistant cells survive and spread the plasmid vertically and by conjugation","The plasmid is destroyed by the outer membrane","The bacterium converts into a dormant spore and in doing so loses the plasmid permanently","The resistance gene is diluted out within one generation","The LPS neutralises the antibiotic outside the cell"],"e":"Plasmids carry dozens of genes including antibiotic-resistance genes, replicate independently and are passed to progeny (vertical) and sometimes between bacteria by conjugation (horizontal). Under antibiotic pressure resistant cells survive, which is why resistance spreads, as with MRSA and VRE.","w":["The outer membrane does not destroy plasmids.","Gram-negative rods named are not spore-formers.","Plasmids are passed to progeny.","Not the mechanism described."],"k":"Plasmid resistance spreads vertically and horizontally.","rn":[["","What are the 2 ways plasmids are passed on?","What are the 2 ways plasmids are passed on?","To daughter cells (vertical); Between bacteria (horizontal, conjugation)"]]},{"id":34121,"t":"Bacterial Classification","s":"A 65-year-old man in hospital develops a fever and an infected wound. A swab is Gram stained: crystal violet and iodine are applied, then alcohol, then safranin. The bacteria appear purple, round and arranged in clusters.<br><br>What is the most likely organism, and why did it stay purple?","o":["Staphylococcus (e.g. S. aureus); a thick peptidoglycan wall retains crystal violet","Streptococcus; a thin peptidoglycan wall with an outer membrane","E. coli; lipopolysaccharide holds the purple stain","Neisseria; Gram-negative cocci stay purple after alcohol","Mycobacterium tuberculosis; its mycolic acid layer makes it stain strongly purple on Gram stain"],"e":"Step 1: Gram-positive bacteria have a thick peptidoglycan wall that keeps the crystal violet–iodine stain after decolourising with alcohol, so they appear purple; Gram negatives (thin peptidoglycan plus an outer membrane) lose it and take up the pink safranin. Step 2: purple cocci in clusters are staphylococci (streptococci form chains), and S. aureus is a common nosocomial wound pathogen.","w":["Streptococci form chains, and thin walls with outer membranes are Gram negative.","E. coli is a Gram-negative rod and would be pink.","Neisseria are Gram-negative and would be pink.","Mycobacteria do not Gram stain."],"k":"Purple cocci in clusters = staphylococci (thick peptidoglycan).","rn":[["","Gram staining: first 2 steps?","Gram staining: first 2 steps?","Dry on glass plate; Crystal violet, set with iodine"],["","Gram positive vs Gram negative: peptidoglycan?","Gram positive vs Gram negative: peptidoglycan?","Positive: thick; Negative: thin"],["","What cell wall do mycobacteria have?","What cell wall do mycobacteria have?","Thick, Gram positive-like; Thick lipid layer; Mycolic acid mycomembrane"]]},{"id":34122,"t":"Bacterial Envelope and Endotoxin","s":"A 70-year-old woman with a urinary infection caused by a Gram-negative rod develops a sudden high fever and a severe inflammatory response. Her doctor explains that a molecule from the bacterial outer membrane, rather than a secreted toxin, is driving the reaction.<br><br>Which molecule is this, and which part is responsible?","o":["Endotoxin (lipopolysaccharide); the lipid A component","Exotoxin; secreted by Gram-positive bacteria only","Peptidoglycan; the NAM sugar","The capsule; its polysaccharide coat","Teichoic acid of the outer membrane, which is found only in Gram-negative bacteria"],"e":"Step 1: endotoxin is the lipopolysaccharide (LPS) of the outer membrane of Gram-negative bacteria; \"endo\" means it is part of the bacterium, unlike exotoxins that are made and exported. Step 2: the lipid A component of LPS is toxic and can trigger a strong inflammatory response in the host.","w":["Exotoxins are exported, and both Gram types make them.","Peptidoglycan is not the endotoxin.","The capsule hides the cell wall; it is not endotoxin.","Teichoic acids are Gram-positive features."],"k":"Endotoxin = LPS (lipid A) of Gram negatives → strong inflammation.","rn":[["","Endotoxin","Endotoxin","Lipopolysaccharide; Of Gram negative outer membrane"],["","Which part of Lipopolysaccharide is the toxic part?","Which part of Lipopolysaccharide is the toxic part?","Lipid A"]]},{"id":34123,"t":"Koch's Postulates","s":"A researcher believes a newly found bacterium causes a lung disease in cattle. She finds it in every sick animal, grows it in pure culture, and injects it into healthy animals, which then develop the disease.<br><br>What must she do next to complete Koch's postulates?","o":["Isolate the same organism again from the newly infected animals","Show that the organism has a capsule","Treat the animals with an antibiotic","Show the organism is Gram positive","Grow it alongside other bacteria to prove that it can survive in a mixed culture outside the host"],"e":"Step 1: Koch's postulates are: the microorganism is always associated with the disease; it can be isolated and grown in pure culture; the cultured microbe causes disease in a healthy animal; and the same organism can be isolated from the newly infected animal. Step 2: she has done the first three, so the final step is re-isolating the same organism from the newly infected animals.","w":["A capsule is not part of Koch's postulates.","Treatment is not one of the postulates.","Gram stain is not part of the postulates.","The postulates require pure, not mixed, culture."],"k":"Koch: associated → pure culture → causes disease → re-isolated."},{"id":34124,"t":"Peptidoglycan and Mycobacteria","s":"A drug company develops an antibiotic that stops bacteria cross-linking the peptide side-chains of their cell wall polymer. The drug kills many bacteria but has no effect on human cells. A colleague asks why it is so selective.<br><br>Which enzyme does the drug likely block, and why is it safe for human cells?","o":["Transpeptidase; peptidoglycan synthesis is unique to bacteria","Transposase; humans have no DNA","Reverse transcriptase; bacteria are viruses","DNA polymerase; human cells have no polymerase","Lipid A synthase; human cells have the same outer membrane as Gram-negative bacteria"],"e":"Step 1: peptidoglycan is a 3-D polymer of NAG and NAM sugars with 3–5 amino acid peptides, cross-linked by transpeptidase enzymes. Step 2: peptidoglycan synthetic pathways are unique to bacteria, so every step (sugar polymerisation, side-chain elongation, transpeptidase cross-linking) can be targeted by antibiotics without harming human cells.","w":["Transposase moves transposons, not wall cross-links.","Bacteria are not viruses.","Human cells do have DNA polymerase.","Human cells do not have an LPS outer membrane."],"k":"Transpeptidase cross-links peptidoglycan; unique to bacteria → antibiotic target.","rn":[["","Why is peptidoglycan synthesis a good antibiotic target?","Why is peptidoglycan synthesis a good antibiotic target?","Unique to bacteria; Does not affect our cells"],["","What are the 3 steps of peptidoglycan synthesis?","What are the 3 steps of peptidoglycan synthesis?","Sugars polymerise (backbone); Peptide side-chains elongate; Transpeptidase cross-links"]]},{"id":34125,"t":"Peptidoglycan and Mycobacteria","s":"A 40-year-old man has a chronic cough, night sweats and weight loss. His sputum Gram stain shows no clearly stained organisms, but a special acid-fast stain reveals rod-shaped bacteria. The organism survives inside the patient's immune cells.<br><br>Which organism is most likely, and why does it not Gram stain?","o":["Mycobacterium tuberculosis; a thick mycolic acid layer","Mycoplasma pneumoniae; it has no cell wall","Haemophilus influenzae; its capsule hides the wall","E. coli; its outer membrane repels stains","Clostridium; its endospores cannot take up any stain from the Gram stain procedure"],"e":"Step 1: mycobacteria have basically a Gram-positive cell wall but with a very thick lipid mycomembrane (mycolic acid) anchored to the peptidoglycan, so they do not Gram stain and need an acid-fast stain. Step 2: this layer aids intracellular survival; M. tuberculosis (proved by Koch to cause TB) fits the cough, night sweats and weight loss.","w":["Mycoplasma lack a wall, but this organism is acid fast.","H. influenzae is a Gram-negative rod that Gram stains.","E. coli is a Gram-negative rod that Gram stains pink.","Clostridium is a Gram-positive rod that Gram stains."],"k":"Mycobacteria: mycolic acid wall → no Gram stain → acid fast.","rn":[["","What cell wall do mycobacteria have?","What cell wall do mycobacteria have?","Thick, Gram positive-like; Thick lipid layer; Mycolic acid mycomembrane"]]},{"id":34126,"t":"Other Bacterial Features","s":"A hospital ward has an outbreak of Clostridium difficile diarrhoea. Despite cleaning with standard alcohol gel and disinfectants, cases continue. The infection control team explains that this organism can survive in a dormant form on surfaces for long periods.<br><br>What form allows this, and what are its properties?","o":["Spores: dormant, metabolically inactive, resistant to drying, heat, disinfection and digestion","Capsules: polysaccharide coats that make bacteria grow faster","Plasmids: DNA rings that make bacteria resistant to all antibiotics","Biofilms of lipid A that stop the bacteria dividing","Exponential phase cells that grow rapidly on dry surfaces and are killed easily by alcohol"],"e":"Step 1: some medically important bacteria, Bacillus and Clostridium species, make spores, a non-replicating dormant form that is metabolically inactive. Step 2: spores are resistant to drying, temperature, disinfection and digestion, which is important in the clinical disease pattern and infection control.","w":["Capsules hide the cell wall; they are not dormant forms.","Plasmids carry genes but are not a survival form.","Lipid A is part of LPS, not a survival form.","Spores are dormant, not growing, and resist disinfection."],"k":"Spores (Bacillus, Clostridium): dormant, resist drying, heat, disinfection.","rn":[["","What are the 4 things spores resist?","What are the 4 things spores resist?","Drying; Temperature; Disinfection; Digestion"],["","What is a spore?","What is a spore?","Non-replicating; dormant form; Metabolically inactive"]]},{"id":34127,"t":"Other Bacterial Features","s":"An antibiotic-resistant strain of bacteria appears on a ward. Researchers find that the resistance gene sits on a small circular piece of DNA outside the chromosome, and that it has spread to a different species through direct contact between bacteria. The gene can also hop between this DNA and the chromosome.<br><br>Which elements and process explain this?","o":["A plasmid spread by conjugation, with a transposon moving the gene","A spore spread by digestion, with a capsule","70S ribosomes spread by binary fission","Lipopolysaccharide spread by the O antigen","A capsule transferred by contact, with endotoxin carrying the resistance gene into the chromosome"],"e":"Step 1: plasmids are circular, independently replicating extra-chromosomal DNA that can be passed to progeny (vertical) or between bacteria by conjugation (horizontal gene transfer). Step 2: transposons are DNA sequences encoding transposase that can move between genomic and plasmid DNA; both can carry toxin and antibiotic-resistance genes.","w":["Spores are dormant forms and do not transfer genes.","Ribosomes make protein; they do not carry resistance genes.","LPS is endotoxin, not a gene carrier.","Capsules and endotoxin do not carry genes."],"k":"Plasmids (conjugation) + transposons spread resistance genes.","rn":[["","What can transposons move between?","What can transposons move between?","Genomic DNA and plasmids; Plasmid and genomic DNA"],["","What are the 2 ways plasmids are passed on?","What are the 2 ways plasmids are passed on?","To daughter cells (vertical); Between bacteria (horizontal, conjugation)"]]},{"id":34128,"t":"Other Bacterial Features","s":"A 2-year-old unvaccinated child develops a severe invasive infection with Haemophilus influenzae. The organism has a thick polysaccharide coat around its cell wall. Studies show that protective immunity depends on antibodies against this coat rather than against the cell wall.<br><br>Why does the coat make the organism more dangerous?","o":["The capsule hides the immunogenic cell wall, conferring virulence","The capsule is endotoxin, which kills cells directly","The capsule makes the bacteria Gram positive","The capsule is a spore that resists digestion","The capsule lowers the metabolic burden, so the bacteria grow much faster than other strains"],"e":"Step 1: the capsule is a polysaccharide coat that \"hides\" the immunogenic cell wall, so immunity requires antibodies to the capsule. Step 2: it confers virulence, e.g. in Haemophilus influenzae, although it is a metabolic burden on the bacterium.","w":["Endotoxin is LPS, not the capsule.","H. influenzae is Gram negative; the capsule does not change this.","A capsule is not a spore.","The capsule is a metabolic burden, not a benefit to growth."],"k":"Capsule hides the cell wall → virulence (H. influenzae).","rn":[["","What does the capsule hide?","What does the capsule hide?","Immunogenic cell wall"]]},{"id":34129,"t":"Bacterial Growth","s":"A lab inoculates broth with bacteria. For the first hour cell numbers do not rise. They then double steadily for several hours. Later, as nutrients run low and waste builds up, numbers stop rising, and finally they fall. A clinician notes that one phase resembles a walled-off abscess.<br><br>Which phases are these, and which resembles an abscess?","o":["Lag, exponential, stationary, death; stationary ≈ abscess","Exponential, lag, death, stationary; lag ≈ abscess","Lag, stationary, exponential, death; exponential ≈ abscess","Death, lag, exponential, stationary; death ≈ abscess","Lag, exponential, stationary, death; exponential phase ≈ abscess, as cells double rapidly"],"e":"Step 1: the growth curve shows a lag phase (no increase, adjustment), exponential (log) phase (doubling), stationary phase (nutrients depleted, metabolites build up, division stops) and death phase (resources exhausted, toxic environment). Step 2: gene regulation links phases to disease: exponential ≈ superficial infection/bacteraemia, while stationary ≈ abscess.","w":["The order is wrong.","Stationary follows exponential.","The first phase is lag.","Exponential is linked to superficial infection/bacteraemia."],"k":"Lag → exponential → stationary → death; stationary ≈ abscess.","rn":[["","What are the 4 phases of the bacterial growth curve?","What are the 4 phases of the bacterial growth curve?","Lag; Exponential (log); Stationary; Death"],["","Exponential vs stationary phase: matching infection?","Exponential vs stationary phase: matching infection?","Exponential: superficial infection / bacteraemia; Stationary: abscess"]]},{"id":34130,"t":"Bacterial Growth","s":"A wound swab is cultured in two conditions. Organism X grows only when oxygen is completely excluded and uses fermentation; oxygen is toxic to it. Organism Y grows both with and without oxygen. Organism Z needs oxygen as its final electron acceptor.<br><br>Which atmospheric categories are X, Y and Z?","o":["X: obligate anaerobe; Y: facultative anaerobe; Z: aerobe","X: aerobe; Y: obligate anaerobe; Z: facultative anaerobe","X: facultative anaerobe; Y: aerobe; Z: obligate anaerobe","All three are facultative anaerobes","X: aerobe that ferments; Y: obligate anaerobe that tolerates oxygen; Z: facultative anaerobe"],"e":"Step 1: obligate anaerobes use fermentation (final electron acceptor is an organic molecule) and oxygen is usually toxic to them, while aerobes use O₂ as the final electron acceptor. Step 2: facultative anaerobes can switch between aerobic and anaerobic metabolism, growing with or without oxygen.","w":["The categories are mismatched.","X cannot grow with oxygen, so it is obligate.","Only Y grows both ways.","Aerobes use oxygen, and obligate anaerobes are harmed by it."],"k":"Obligate anaerobe: O₂ toxic; facultative: both; aerobe: needs O₂.","rn":[["","What is a facultative anaerobe?","What is a facultative anaerobe?","Switches between; Aerobic and anaerobic"]]}]);
